) VALUES (
  'Autonomic Pharmacology: Complete Study Notes',
  'autonomic-pharmacology',
  'Autonomic pharmacology notes with a receptor and end-organ effect table, the four drug classes with prototype drugs, and clinical pearls on anaphylaxis, myasthenia and organophosphate poisoning.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These notes organize the autonomic nervous system for pharmacology exams: a receptor table with end-organ effects, the four drug classes with prototype agents, and the clinical pearls — anaphylaxis, glaucoma, myasthenia and organophosphate poisoning — that make the topic stick."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Autonomic pharmacology looks like a wall of drug names, but the whole subject hangs on one receptor table. Learn what each receptor does to each organ, and every drug in the class becomes predictable: an agonist at that receptor reproduces the effect, a blocker removes it. The "},{"type":"text","text":"pain pathways topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/pain-pathways/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers where these autonomic fibres run and why sympathetic blocks relieve some pain syndromes."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Receptor Table"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Receptor"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Location"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Effect"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Transduction"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha-1"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vascular smooth muscle, internal sphincters, radial muscle of iris"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vasoconstriction, sphincter contraction, mydriasis"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gq — IP3, calcium"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha-2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Presynaptic terminals, CNS"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inhibits norepinephrine release; lowers sympathetic outflow"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gi — decreased cAMP"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta-1"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Heart, kidney (juxtaglomerular cells)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Increased rate, force and conduction; renin release"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gs — increased cAMP"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta-2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bronchi, skeletal muscle vessels, uterus, liver"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bronchodilation, vasodilation, uterine relaxation, glycogenolysis"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gs — increased cAMP"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta-3"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adipose tissue, bladder"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipolysis; bladder relaxation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gs"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nicotinic (NN)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ganglionic transmission, catecholamine release"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ion channel"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nicotinic (NM)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Neuromuscular junction"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Skeletal muscle contraction"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ion channel"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Muscarinic M2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Heart"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bradycardia, decreased conduction"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gi"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Muscarinic M3"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Smooth muscle, glands, eye"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contraction of gut and bladder detrusor, secretions, miosis and accommodation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gq"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The single most examinable pattern: "},{"type":"text","text":"sympathetic stimulation speeds the heart (beta-1) and constricts skin and splanchnic vessels (alpha-1) but dilates skeletal muscle vessels (beta-2)","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; "},{"type":"text","text":"parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart (M2) and constricts bronchi and gut glands (M3)","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sympathomimetics"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direct-acting, catecholamines:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"- "},{"type":"text","text":"Epinephrine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — all receptors; anaphylaxis, resuscitation, prolongs     local anesthesia.   - "},{"type":"text","text":"Norepinephrine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — alpha-1 and beta-1 (negligible beta-2): a     vasoconstricting pressor agent in septic shock.   - "},{"type":"text","text":"Dopamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — dose-dependent: renal and splanchnic vasodilation at low     dose (D1), beta-1 then alpha-1 as the dose rises.   - "},{"type":"text","text":"Isoproterenol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — pure beta; historical teaching tool.   - "},{"type":"text","text":"Dobutamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — beta-1 selective inotrope for acute heart failure."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Direct-acting, non-catecholamines:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"phenylephrine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (pure alpha-1 —"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"nasal decongestant, mydriatic without cycloplegia), "},{"type":"text","text":"albuterol/salbutamol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"   (beta-2 — asthma), "},{"type":"text","text":"clonidine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (alpha-2 — antihypertensive, also used as   an adjunct in pain management)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Indirect-acting:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"amphetamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — displaces norepinephrine from"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"terminals; also releases dopamine and serotonin (ADHD, narcolepsy)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mixed:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ephedrine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — direct plus indirect, orally active, crosses"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"into the CNS; used for hypotension in obstetric spinal anesthesia."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sympatholytics"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha-1 blockers:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"phenoxybenzamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (irreversible —"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"pheochromocytoma), "},{"type":"text","text":"phentolamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (reversible), "},{"type":"text","text":"prazosin","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and   "},{"type":"text","text":"tamsulosin","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (uroselective — benign prostatic hyperplasia). Adverse   effect: first-dose orthostatic hypotension."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha-2 blockers:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" rarely therapeutic; phentolamine has some effect."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta blockers:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"propranolol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (non-selective — hypertension,"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"arrhythmias, migraine prophylaxis, thyrotoxicosis), "},{"type":"text","text":"metoprolol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and   "},{"type":"text","text":"atenolol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (beta-1 selective — safer in asthma), "},{"type":"text","text":"carvedilol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and   "},{"type":"text","text":"labetalol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (mixed alpha-beta). Core adverse effects: bradycardia,   bronchospasm, fatigue, masking of hypoglycemia."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Metyrosine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (blocks catecholamine synthesis) completes the class at"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"textbook level."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parasympathomimetics"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Choline esters:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"bethanechol","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — M3 selective; urinary retention and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"gastrointestinal atony without cardiovascular effect. "},{"type":"text","text":"Methacholine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" —   bronchial provocation testing in asthma diagnosis."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Natural alkaloids:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pilocarpine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the classic drug for open-angle"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"glaucoma (contracts ciliary muscle, improves outflow) and for xerostomia.   "},{"type":"text","text":"Muscarine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the mushroom poison, a teaching toxidrome."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anticholinesterases (indirect):","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"- "},{"type":"text","text":"Reversible:","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" edrophonium (ultra-short, diagnostic in myasthenia),     neostigmine and pyridostigmine (myasthenia gravis; neostigmine also     reverses non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockade), physostigmine     (crosses the blood-brain barrier — central anticholinergic syndrome),     donepezil (CNS — Alzheimer disease).   - "},{"type":"text","text":"Irreversible:","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" organophosphates — echothiophate as an antiquated     glaucoma drug, and the insecticides and nerve agents of poisoning     cases."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anticholinergics"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atropine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — bradycardia, pre-anesthetic secretion control, organophosphate"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"antidote; ophthalmic use has largely given way to tropicamide."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scopolamine","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — motion sickness, transdermal."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ipratropium","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"tiotropium","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — inhaled, bronchodilator in COPD without"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"systemic effect."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — overactive bladder."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Glycopyrrolate","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — perioperative and with neostigmine, quaternary and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CNS-sparing."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tropicamide, cyclopentolate","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — mydriasis and cycloplegia for fundoscopy."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anticholinergic toxidrome:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" dry as a bone (anhidrosis), red as a beet (flushing), hot as a hare (hyperthermia), blind as a bat (mydriasis with cycloplegia), mad as a hatter (delirium), plus urinary retention and tachycardia — and the bowel is silent."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinical Pearls and Worked Framing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A viva favourite runs like this: "},{"type":"text","text":"a patient on timolol eye drops for glaucoma develops wheeze — explain.","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" Timolol is a non-selective beta blocker absorbed systemically from the conjunctiva; beta-2 blockade in the bronchi precipitates asthma. The fix is switching to a prostaglandin analogue or pilocarpine. This single vignette tests receptor selectivity, route of administration, and end-organ prediction at once — the entire subject in one question."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Then the classics: anaphylaxis gets intramuscular "},{"type":"text","text":"epinephrine","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", never an antihistamine first; myasthenia gets "},{"type":"text","text":"pyridostigmine","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with edrophonium or the ice-pack test to diagnose; organophosphate exposure gets "},{"type":"text","text":"atropine plus pralidoxime","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; and clonidine, the alpha-2 agonist, shows up twice — as an antihypertensive that must never be stopped abruptly, and as an epidural or systemic adjunct in chronic pain, where it dampens sympathetic traffic."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revision Pointers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rebuild the receptor table from memory, then walk through each drug class asking \"which receptors does this agent touch?\" — the answer predicts everything. Test yourself with the "},{"type":"text","text":"pharmacology practice quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/pharmacology/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", and pair this with the "},{"type":"text","text":"brachial plexus study note","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/notes/brachial-plexus/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" when revising the anatomy of the peripheral nervous system."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Learning objectives"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classify adrenergic and cholinergic receptors with their transduction and end-organ effects"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Predict the end-organ response to agonists and antagonists at each receptor"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List sympathomimetics, sympatholytics, parasympathomimetics and anticholinergics with prototypes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explain the clinical use of epinephrine in anaphylaxis and neostigmine in myasthenia gravis"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recognize the toxidromes of anticholinergic and organophosphate poisoning"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Describe the role of alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine in analgesia and sympathetic control"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key terms"}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sympathomimetic","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A drug that mimics sympathetic activity, either directly at receptors or indirectly by releasing norepinephrine."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sympatholytic","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A drug that blocks sympathetic activity, most importantly alpha and beta receptor antagonists."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cholinomimetic","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A drug that mimics parasympathetic activity, either at muscarinic receptors or by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anticholinesterase","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A drug that inhibits acetylcholinesterase, raising acetylcholine at all cholinergic junctions; reversible (neostigmine) or irreversible (organophosphates)."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Anticholinergic","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A drug that blocks muscarinic receptors — atropine and its derivatives — producing dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and tachycardia."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Catecholamine","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A transmitter with the catechol nucleus — norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine — all short-acting and not orally active."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mydriasis-cycloplegia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Pupillary dilation with loss of accommodation, produced by anticholinergic (atropine-like) agents in the eye."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Orthostatic hypotension","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A fall in blood pressure on standing from loss of alpha-1 mediated venoconstriction — a key adverse effect of alpha blockers."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Important points"}]},{"type":"important","content":[{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta-1 dominates the heart (rate, force, conduction); beta-2 relaxes bronchial and vascular smooth muscle; alpha-1 constricts vessels and the internal sphincters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Muscarinic (M3) stimulation contracts smooth muscle and glands; M2 slows the heart."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Epinephrine intramuscularly is the first-line drug in anaphylaxis — it reverses the bronchoconstriction and vasodilation simultaneously."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Never give a beta blocker alone in pheochromocytoma — unopposed alpha vasoconstriction can precipitate a hypertensive crisis; alpha blockade (phenoxybenzamine) comes first."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Neostigmine does not cross the blood-brain barrier and is used in myasthenia; physostigmine crosses it and treats central anticholinergic syndrome."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atropine is the antidote for organophosphate poisoning after pralidoxime; pralidoxime reactivates the enzyme before aging occurs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine reduce sympathetic outflow centrally and are used as antihypertensives and adjuncts in pain management."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Autonomic pharmacology is organized around six receptors — alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1, beta-2, nicotinic and muscarinic — whose end-organ effects can be predicted from a single table. Sympathomimetics run from epinephrine and dopamine through indirect agents like amphetamine; sympatholytics from non-selective propranolol to cardioselective metoprolol and alpha-1 selective tamsulosin. Cholinomimetics include the muscarinic agonist bethanechol, the antiglaucoma alkaloid pilocarpine, and the anticholinesterases neostigmine and pyridostigmine for myasthenia gravis; atropine and its derivatives block muscarinic effects and treat organophosphate poisoning, bradycardia and secretions, at the price of the classic anticholinergic toxidrome."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is epinephrine the drug of choice in anaphylaxis?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It acts at both alpha and beta receptors at once: alpha-1 vasoconstriction restores blood pressure and reduces angioedema, beta-2 relaxes bronchi, and beta-1 supports cardiac output. It is given intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh; no other single drug covers all the life-threatening features."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why must alpha blockade precede beta blockade in pheochromocytoma?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta blockers remove beta-2 mediated vasodilation in skeletal muscle while leaving the tumor''s massive catecholamine output acting on alpha-1 receptors — unopposed vasoconstriction can trigger a hypertensive crisis or pulmonary edema. Phenoxybenzamine is started first, and only then is a beta blocker added."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is neostigmine preferred to physostigmine in myasthenia gravis?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Neostigmine is a quaternary amine that does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it amplifies acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction without central effects. Pyridostigmine, longer-acting, is the usual maintenance drug; physostigmine crosses into the CNS and is reserved for central anticholinergic poisoning."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What are the cardinal features of organophosphate poisoning?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cholinergic toxidrome of DUMBBELLS — diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhea and bronchospasm, bradycardia, excitation, lacrimation, lethargy, salivation and sweating. Treat with atropine for the muscarinic effects plus pralidoxime to reactivate the enzyme, which must be given before the phosphate-enzyme bond ages."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Katzung''s Basic & Clinical Pharmacology","source":"McGraw Hill"},{"title":"Rang & Dale''s Pharmacology","source":"Elsevier"},{"title":"Goodman & Gilman''s The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics","source":"McGraw Hill"}]}}]}',
  '<h2>Overview</h2><p>These notes organize the autonomic nervous system for pharmacology exams: a receptor table with end-organ effects, the four drug classes with prototype agents, and the clinical pearls — anaphylaxis, glaucoma, myasthenia and organophosphate poisoning — that make the topic stick.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Autonomic pharmacology looks like a wall of drug names, but the whole subject hangs on one receptor table. Learn what each receptor does to each organ, and every drug in the class becomes predictable: an agonist at that receptor reproduces the effect, a blocker removes it. The <a href="/topics/pain-pathways/" rel="noopener">pain pathways topic hub</a> covers where these autonomic fibres run and why sympathetic blocks relieve some pain syndromes.</p><h2>The Receptor Table</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Receptor</p></th><th><p>Location</p></th><th><p>Effect</p></th><th><p>Transduction</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Alpha-1</p></td><td><p>Vascular smooth muscle, internal sphincters, radial muscle of iris</p></td><td><p>Vasoconstriction, sphincter contraction, mydriasis</p></td><td><p>Gq — IP3, calcium</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Alpha-2</p></td><td><p>Presynaptic terminals, CNS</p></td><td><p>Inhibits norepinephrine release; lowers sympathetic outflow</p></td><td><p>Gi — decreased cAMP</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Beta-1</p></td><td><p>Heart, kidney (juxtaglomerular cells)</p></td><td><p>Increased rate, force and conduction; renin release</p></td><td><p>Gs — increased cAMP</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Beta-2</p></td><td><p>Bronchi, skeletal muscle vessels, uterus, liver</p></td><td><p>Bronchodilation, vasodilation, uterine relaxation, glycogenolysis</p></td><td><p>Gs — increased cAMP</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Beta-3</p></td><td><p>Adipose tissue, bladder</p></td><td><p>Lipolysis; bladder relaxation</p></td><td><p>Gs</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Nicotinic (NN)</p></td><td><p>Autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla</p></td><td><p>Ganglionic transmission, catecholamine release</p></td><td><p>Ion channel</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Nicotinic (NM)</p></td><td><p>Neuromuscular junction</p></td><td><p>Skeletal muscle contraction</p></td><td><p>Ion channel</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Muscarinic M2</p></td><td><p>Heart</p></td><td><p>Bradycardia, decreased conduction</p></td><td><p>Gi</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Muscarinic M3</p></td><td><p>Smooth muscle, glands, eye</p></td><td><p>Contraction of gut and bladder detrusor, secretions, miosis and accommodation</p></td><td><p>Gq</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The single most examinable pattern: <strong>sympathetic stimulation speeds the heart (beta-1) and constricts skin and splanchnic vessels (alpha-1) but dilates skeletal muscle vessels (beta-2)</strong>; <strong>parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart (M2) and constricts bronchi and gut glands (M3)</strong>.</p><h2>Sympathomimetics</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Direct-acting, catecholamines:</strong></p></li></ul><p>- <em>Epinephrine</em> — all receptors; anaphylaxis, resuscitation, prolongs     local anesthesia.   - <em>Norepinephrine</em> — alpha-1 and beta-1 (negligible beta-2): a     vasoconstricting pressor agent in septic shock.   - <em>Dopamine</em> — dose-dependent: renal and splanchnic vasodilation at low     dose (D1), beta-1 then alpha-1 as the dose rises.   - <em>Isoproterenol</em> — pure beta; historical teaching tool.   - <em>Dobutamine</em> — beta-1 selective inotrope for acute heart failure.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Direct-acting, non-catecholamines:</strong> <em>phenylephrine</em> (pure alpha-1 —</p></li></ul><p>nasal decongestant, mydriatic without cycloplegia), <em>albuterol/salbutamol</em>   (beta-2 — asthma), <em>clonidine</em> (alpha-2 — antihypertensive, also used as   an adjunct in pain management).</p><ul><li><p><strong>Indirect-acting:</strong> <em>amphetamine</em> — displaces norepinephrine from</p></li></ul><p>terminals; also releases dopamine and serotonin (ADHD, narcolepsy).</p><ul><li><p><strong>Mixed:</strong> <em>ephedrine</em> — direct plus indirect, orally active, crosses</p></li></ul><p>into the CNS; used for hypotension in obstetric spinal anesthesia.</p><h2>Sympatholytics</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Alpha-1 blockers:</strong> <em>phenoxybenzamine</em> (irreversible —</p></li></ul><p>pheochromocytoma), <em>phentolamine</em> (reversible), <em>prazosin</em> and   <em>tamsulosin</em> (uroselective — benign prostatic hyperplasia). Adverse   effect: first-dose orthostatic hypotension.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Alpha-2 blockers:</strong> rarely therapeutic; phentolamine has some effect.</p></li><li><p><strong>Beta blockers:</strong> <em>propranolol</em> (non-selective — hypertension,</p></li></ul><p>arrhythmias, migraine prophylaxis, thyrotoxicosis), <em>metoprolol</em> and   <em>atenolol</em> (beta-1 selective — safer in asthma), <em>carvedilol</em> and   <em>labetalol</em> (mixed alpha-beta). Core adverse effects: bradycardia,   bronchospasm, fatigue, masking of hypoglycemia.</p><ul><li><p><em>Metyrosine</em> (blocks catecholamine synthesis) completes the class at</p></li></ul><p>textbook level.</p><h2>Parasympathomimetics</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Choline esters:</strong> <em>bethanechol</em> — M3 selective; urinary retention and</p></li></ul><p>gastrointestinal atony without cardiovascular effect. <em>Methacholine</em> —   bronchial provocation testing in asthma diagnosis.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Natural alkaloids:</strong> <em>pilocarpine</em> — the classic drug for open-angle</p></li></ul><p>glaucoma (contracts ciliary muscle, improves outflow) and for xerostomia.   <em>Muscarine</em> — the mushroom poison, a teaching toxidrome.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Anticholinesterases (indirect):</strong></p></li></ul><p>- <em>Reversible:</em> edrophonium (ultra-short, diagnostic in myasthenia),     neostigmine and pyridostigmine (myasthenia gravis; neostigmine also     reverses non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockade), physostigmine     (crosses the blood-brain barrier — central anticholinergic syndrome),     donepezil (CNS — Alzheimer disease).   - <em>Irreversible:</em> organophosphates — echothiophate as an antiquated     glaucoma drug, and the insecticides and nerve agents of poisoning     cases.</p><h2>Anticholinergics</h2><ul><li><p><em>Atropine</em> — bradycardia, pre-anesthetic secretion control, organophosphate</p></li></ul><p>antidote; ophthalmic use has largely given way to tropicamide.</p><ul><li><p><em>Scopolamine</em> — motion sickness, transdermal.</p></li><li><p><em>Ipratropium</em> and <em>tiotropium</em> — inhaled, bronchodilator in COPD without</p></li></ul><p>systemic effect.</p><ul><li><p><em>Oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin</em> — overactive bladder.</p></li><li><p><em>Glycopyrrolate</em> — perioperative and with neostigmine, quaternary and</p></li></ul><p>CNS-sparing.</p><ul><li><p><em>Tropicamide, cyclopentolate</em> — mydriasis and cycloplegia for fundoscopy.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Anticholinergic toxidrome:</strong> dry as a bone (anhidrosis), red as a beet (flushing), hot as a hare (hyperthermia), blind as a bat (mydriasis with cycloplegia), mad as a hatter (delirium), plus urinary retention and tachycardia — and the bowel is silent.</p><h2>Clinical Pearls and Worked Framing</h2><p>A viva favourite runs like this: <em>a patient on timolol eye drops for glaucoma develops wheeze — explain.</em> Timolol is a non-selective beta blocker absorbed systemically from the conjunctiva; beta-2 blockade in the bronchi precipitates asthma. The fix is switching to a prostaglandin analogue or pilocarpine. This single vignette tests receptor selectivity, route of administration, and end-organ prediction at once — the entire subject in one question.</p><p>Then the classics: anaphylaxis gets intramuscular <strong>epinephrine</strong>, never an antihistamine first; myasthenia gets <strong>pyridostigmine</strong> with edrophonium or the ice-pack test to diagnose; organophosphate exposure gets <strong>atropine plus pralidoxime</strong>; and clonidine, the alpha-2 agonist, shows up twice — as an antihypertensive that must never be stopped abruptly, and as an epidural or systemic adjunct in chronic pain, where it dampens sympathetic traffic.</p><h2>Revision Pointers</h2><p>Rebuild the receptor table from memory, then walk through each drug class asking "which receptors does this agent touch?" — the answer predicts everything. Test yourself with the <a href="/practice/quiz/pharmacology/" rel="noopener">pharmacology practice quiz</a>, and pair this with the <a href="/notes/brachial-plexus/" rel="noopener">brachial plexus study note</a> when revising the anatomy of the peripheral nervous system.</p><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li><p>Classify adrenergic and cholinergic receptors with their transduction and end-organ effects</p></li><li><p>Predict the end-organ response to agonists and antagonists at each receptor</p></li><li><p>List sympathomimetics, sympatholytics, parasympathomimetics and anticholinergics with prototypes</p></li><li><p>Explain the clinical use of epinephrine in anaphylaxis and neostigmine in myasthenia gravis</p></li><li><p>Recognize the toxidromes of anticholinergic and organophosphate poisoning</p></li><li><p>Describe the role of alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine in analgesia and sympathetic control</p></li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Sympathomimetic</strong> — A drug that mimics sympathetic activity, either directly at receptors or indirectly by releasing norepinephrine.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Sympatholytic</strong> — A drug that blocks sympathetic activity, most importantly alpha and beta receptor antagonists.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Cholinomimetic</strong> — A drug that mimics parasympathetic activity, either at muscarinic receptors or by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Anticholinesterase</strong> — A drug that inhibits acetylcholinesterase, raising acetylcholine at all cholinergic junctions; reversible (neostigmine) or irreversible (organophosphates).</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Anticholinergic</strong> — A drug that blocks muscarinic receptors — atropine and its derivatives — producing dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention and tachycardia.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Catecholamine</strong> — A transmitter with the catechol nucleus — norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine — all short-acting and not orally active.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Mydriasis-cycloplegia</strong> — Pupillary dilation with loss of accommodation, produced by anticholinergic (atropine-like) agents in the eye.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> — A fall in blood pressure on standing from loss of alpha-1 mediated venoconstriction — a key adverse effect of alpha blockers.</p></aside><h2>Important points</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-important" data-type="important"><p class="msn-block-label">Important</p><ul><li><p>Beta-1 dominates the heart (rate, force, conduction); beta-2 relaxes bronchial and vascular smooth muscle; alpha-1 constricts vessels and the internal sphincters.</p></li><li><p>Muscarinic (M3) stimulation contracts smooth muscle and glands; M2 slows the heart.</p></li><li><p>Epinephrine intramuscularly is the first-line drug in anaphylaxis — it reverses the bronchoconstriction and vasodilation simultaneously.</p></li><li><p>Never give a beta blocker alone in pheochromocytoma — unopposed alpha vasoconstriction can precipitate a hypertensive crisis; alpha blockade (phenoxybenzamine) comes first.</p></li><li><p>Neostigmine does not cross the blood-brain barrier and is used in myasthenia; physostigmine crosses it and treats central anticholinergic syndrome.</p></li><li><p>Atropine is the antidote for organophosphate poisoning after pralidoxime; pralidoxime reactivates the enzyme before aging occurs.</p></li><li><p>Alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine reduce sympathetic outflow centrally and are used as antihypertensives and adjuncts in pain management.</p></li></ul></aside><h2>Summary</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p>Autonomic pharmacology is organized around six receptors — alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1, beta-2, nicotinic and muscarinic — whose end-organ effects can be predicted from a single table. Sympathomimetics run from epinephrine and dopamine through indirect agents like amphetamine; sympatholytics from non-selective propranolol to cardioselective metoprolol and alpha-1 selective tamsulosin. Cholinomimetics include the muscarinic agonist bethanechol, the antiglaucoma alkaloid pilocarpine, and the anticholinesterases neostigmine and pyridostigmine for myasthenia gravis; atropine and its derivatives block muscarinic effects and treat organophosphate poisoning, bradycardia and secretions, at the price of the classic anticholinergic toxidrome.</p></aside><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Why is epinephrine the drug of choice in anaphylaxis?</h3><p>It acts at both alpha and beta receptors at once: alpha-1 vasoconstriction restores blood pressure and reduces angioedema, beta-2 relaxes bronchi, and beta-1 supports cardiac output. It is given intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh; no other single drug covers all the life-threatening features.</p><h3>Why must alpha blockade precede beta blockade in pheochromocytoma?</h3><p>Beta blockers remove beta-2 mediated vasodilation in skeletal muscle while leaving the tumor''s massive catecholamine output acting on alpha-1 receptors — unopposed vasoconstriction can trigger a hypertensive crisis or pulmonary edema. Phenoxybenzamine is started first, and only then is a beta blocker added.</p><h3>Why is neostigmine preferred to physostigmine in myasthenia gravis?</h3><p>Neostigmine is a quaternary amine that does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it amplifies acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction without central effects. Pyridostigmine, longer-acting, is the usual maintenance drug; physostigmine crosses into the CNS and is reserved for central anticholinergic poisoning.</p><h3>What are the cardinal features of organophosphate poisoning?</h3><p>The cholinergic toxidrome of DUMBBELLS — diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhea and bronchospasm, bradycardia, excitation, lacrimation, lethargy, salivation and sweating. Treat with atropine for the muscarinic effects plus pralidoxime to reactivate the enzyme, which must be given before the phosphate-enzyme bond ages.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Katzung''s Basic &amp; Clinical Pharmacology. McGraw Hill</li><li>Rang &amp; Dale''s Pharmacology. Elsevier</li><li>Goodman &amp; Gilman''s The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics. McGraw Hill</li></ol></aside>',
