INSERT INTO articles (
  title, slug, excerpt, content_json, content_html, article_type, status, subject_id, topic_id, author_id, published_at, seo_title, meta_description, robots, reading_minutes, created_at, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'Brachial Plexus: Complete Study Notes',
  'brachial-plexus',
  'Brachial plexus study notes covering roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches, with an injuries table for Erb''s palsy, Klumpke''s palsy and radial nerve lesions.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These notes build the brachial plexus level by level — roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches — then apply that map to the classic nerve injuries examiners love: Erb''s palsy, Klumpke''s palsy and wrist drop."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The brachial plexus is the nerve supply of the entire upper limb. It looks intimidating drawn as a diagram, but its logic is simple: five roots combine into trunks, trunks divide, divisions recombine into cords, and cords give the named nerves you test at the bedside. Once you can reproduce that sequence, every classic injury — Erb''s palsy, Klumpke''s palsy, wrist drop — becomes a predictable consequence of where along the plexus the damage sits. For more on how nerve lesions produce the pain patterns students confuse at the bedside, see the "},{"type":"text","text":"pain pathways topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/pain-pathways/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Formation: Roots to Cords"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plexus is formed by the "},{"type":"text","text":"anterior primary rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":". (A contribution from C4 makes it \"prefixed\"; from T2, \"postfixed\" — asked as a one-liner MCQ.)"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roots","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" C5–T1 unite into "},{"type":"text","text":"trunks","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": C5+C6 form the superior trunk, C7"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"continues as the middle trunk, and C8+T1 form the inferior trunk."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each trunk divides into "},{"type":"text","text":"anterior and posterior divisions","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — six in total."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The divisions recombine into "},{"type":"text","text":"cords","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" around the axillary artery, named"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"for their position relative to its second part: anterior divisions of   superior and middle trunks form the "},{"type":"text","text":"lateral cord","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; all three posterior   divisions form the "},{"type":"text","text":"posterior cord","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; the anterior division of the inferior   trunk continues as the "},{"type":"text","text":"medial cord","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Note the exam favourite: the posterior cord receives fibres from "},{"type":"text","text":"all five roots","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", which is why every root value in the plexus contributes to extensor muscles."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Branches Worth Memorizing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Root and trunk branches are small but clinically famous:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (C5) — rhomboids."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long thoracic nerve","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (C5–C7) — serratus anterior; injury causes winged"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"scapula."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Suprascapular nerve","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (C5–C6, from the superior trunk) — supraspinatus"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"and infraspinatus."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cords give the terminal branches:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cord"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Branches"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Root value"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lateral"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lateral pectoral nerve; musculocutaneous nerve; lateral root of median nerve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C5–C7"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Posterior"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upper and lower subscapular nerves; thoracodorsal nerve; axillary nerve; radial nerve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C5–T1"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Medial"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Medial pectoral nerve; medial cutaneous nerves of arm and forearm; ulnar nerve; medial root of median nerve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C8–T1 (ulnar may carry C7)"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The median nerve is formed by lateral (C5–C7) and medial (C8–T1) roots joining on the front of the third part of the axillary artery — the two cords \"shake hands\" over it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Injuries: The Table Examiners Ask For"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Injury"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Site / roots"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Motor loss"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deformity or sign"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Erb''s palsy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upper trunk C5–C6"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deltoid, biceps, supraspinatus, wrist extensors"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Waiter''s tip: adducted, internally rotated arm, extended elbow, pronated forearm, flexed wrist"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Klumpke''s palsy"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lower trunk C8–T1"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intrinsic hand muscles, long flexors"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Claw hand; ± Horner syndrome"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radial nerve at spiral groove"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radial nerve, mid-shaft humerus"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wrist and finger extensors (triceps spared)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wrist drop; sensory loss posterior forearm, dorsum of first web space"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radial nerve in axilla"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radial nerve, axilla"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All radial muscles including triceps"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wrist drop + loss of elbow extension (crutch pressure)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Axillary nerve at surgical neck"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Axillary nerve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deltoid, teres minor"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Loss of shoulder abduction from 15–90°; numb regimental badge area"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long thoracic nerve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C5–C7 on chest wall"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Serratus anterior"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Winged scapula"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worked exam framing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A vignette asks: "},{"type":"text","text":"a newborn after shoulder dystocia holds the arm adducted and internally rotated with a flexed wrist — where is the lesion?","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" That is the waiter''s tip posture, so the answer is the "},{"type":"text","text":"upper trunk (C5–C6), Erb''s palsy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Change the vignette to a fall on the outstretched hand with inability to abduct beyond 15° and numbness over the deltoid, and the answer becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"axillary nerve at the surgical neck of the humerus","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Examiners rarely leave the table above — they only re-dress it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinical Correlations"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Birth injury:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" excessive lateral flexion of the neck during delivery"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"stretches the upper trunk (shoulder dystocia) or tears the lower trunk   (breech delivery pulling the arms). Prognosis of Erb''s palsy is generally   better than Klumpke''s."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thoracic outlet syndrome:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" a cervical rib or tight scalene band"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"compresses the inferior trunk (C8–T1) plus the subclavian artery — pain,   numbness along the ulnar border, and a positive Adson''s test."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Saturday night palsy:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" pressure on the arm against a bench compresses"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"the radial nerve in the spiral groove — the classic cause of painless   wrist drop."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Post-mastectomy weakness:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" injury to the long thoracic nerve during"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"breast surgery is the standard clinical source of winged scapula."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revision Pointers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Draw the plexus from memory before every test — the sequence "},{"type":"text","text":"roots → trunks → divisions → cords → branches","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is worth more marks than any single fact. Then test yourself the forward way (name the branches of the posterior cord) and the backward way (a claw hand with Horner syndrome means which roots?). Finish with the "},{"type":"text","text":"anatomy practice quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/anatomy/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and, for the physiology side of limb nerve function, the "},{"type":"text","text":"cardiac cycle study note","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/notes/cardiac-cycle/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" is a reminder of how the same first-professional pattern of table-plus-vignette questions works in other subjects."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Learning objectives"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trace the formation of the brachial plexus from roots to cords and branches"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Name the branches arising from each cord and their root values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correlate upper and lower trunk injuries with their clinical deformities"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Localize radial, axillary and long thoracic nerve injuries from clinical findings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explain why birth trauma and shoulder dystocia produce specific plexus lesions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apply the plexus map to short-case and MCQ questions on upper limb nerve testing"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key terms"}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roots","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The anterior primary rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1 that unite to form the plexus."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Erb''s palsy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Injury of the upper trunk (C5–C6) producing the waiter''s tip deformity — adducted, internally rotated arm with extended elbow and pronated forearm."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Klumpke''s palsy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Injury of the lower trunk (C8–T1) producing a claw hand, sometimes with Horner syndrome if T1 sympathetic fibres are involved."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cords","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Lateral, posterior and medial cords of the plexus, named for their position relative to the second part of the axillary artery."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wrist drop","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Loss of wrist extension from radial nerve injury, usually at the spiral groove of the humerus."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Winged scapula","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Paralysis of serratus anterior from long thoracic nerve injury (C5–C7); the medial scapular border lifts off the chest wall on pushing."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Horner syndrome","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis and enophthalmos from interruption of T1 sympathetic fibres to the head, seen in some lower trunk lesions."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Regimental badge area","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The skin over the lower deltoid supplied by the axillary nerve; loss of sensation here localizes an axillary nerve injury."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Important points"}]},{"type":"important","content":[{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roots C5–T1 form three trunks, six divisions, three cords and five main terminal branches."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The posterior cord carries fibres from all five roots — every root of the plexus supplies extensor compartments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Erb''s palsy is an upper trunk lesion (C5–C6); Klumpke''s is a lower trunk lesion (C8–T1)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Radial nerve injury at the spiral groove spares triceps but causes wrist drop."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Axillary nerve injury at the surgical neck of the humerus paralyses deltoid and causes loss of sensation over the regimental badge area."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long thoracic nerve injury causes winged scapula; it arises from the roots, not from a cord."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve pierces coracobrachialis; median nerve has no muscles supplied in the arm."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key formulas"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brachial plexus = 5 roots → 3 trunks → 6 divisions → 3 cords → branches","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The branching sequence students must reproduce from memory in one line."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The brachial plexus is formed by the anterior primary rami of C5 to T1, which unite into superior, middle and inferior trunks, each dividing into anterior and posterior divisions that recombine into lateral, posterior and medial cords. The cords give the five terminal branches — musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median and ulnar — and the pattern of injury maps onto this architecture: upper trunk lesions produce Erb''s waiter''s tip deformity, lower trunk lesions produce Klumpke''s claw hand, and individual nerves fail at predictable bony points such as the spiral groove and surgical neck of the humerus."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which nerve roots are involved in Erb''s palsy and what is the deformity?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C5 and C6, the upper trunk. The arm hangs adducted and internally rotated, the elbow is extended, the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed — the waiter''s tip posture. It classically follows shoulder dystocia in delivery or a blow to the shoulder."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why does Klumpke''s palsy sometimes include Horner syndrome?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The lower trunk carries T1 fibres, and T1 also contributes to the sympathetic supply of the head through the stellate ganglion. If the lesion catches these fibres the patient develops ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis on the same side, alongside the claw hand."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is triceps spared when the radial nerve is damaged in the spiral groove?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Branches to triceps leave the radial nerve before it enters the spiral groove on the posterior humerus. A lesion in the groove therefore causes wrist drop and sensory loss over the posterior forearm, but elbow extension is preserved."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the clinical test for long thoracic nerve injury?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask the patient to push against a wall with the arm forward. Serratus anterior paralysis lets the medial border of the scapula lift off the thorax — winged scapula. The nerve (C5–C7) is vulnerable in chest wall surgery and radical mastectomy."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Gray''s Anatomy for Students","source":"Elsevier"},{"title":"Moore''s Clinically Oriented Anatomy","source":"Wolters Kluwer"},{"title":"Snell''s Clinical Anatomy by Regions","source":"Wolters Kluwer"}]}}]}',
  '<h2>Overview</h2><p>These notes build the brachial plexus level by level — roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches — then apply that map to the classic nerve injuries examiners love: Erb''s palsy, Klumpke''s palsy and wrist drop.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>The brachial plexus is the nerve supply of the entire upper limb. It looks intimidating drawn as a diagram, but its logic is simple: five roots combine into trunks, trunks divide, divisions recombine into cords, and cords give the named nerves you test at the bedside. Once you can reproduce that sequence, every classic injury — Erb''s palsy, Klumpke''s palsy, wrist drop — becomes a predictable consequence of where along the plexus the damage sits. For more on how nerve lesions produce the pain patterns students confuse at the bedside, see the <a href="/topics/pain-pathways/" rel="noopener">pain pathways topic hub</a>.</p><h2>Formation: Roots to Cords</h2><p>The plexus is formed by the <strong>anterior primary rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1</strong>. (A contribution from C4 makes it "prefixed"; from T2, "postfixed" — asked as a one-liner MCQ.)</p><ul><li><p><strong>Roots</strong> C5–T1 unite into <strong>trunks</strong>: C5+C6 form the superior trunk, C7</p></li></ul><p>continues as the middle trunk, and C8+T1 form the inferior trunk.</p><ul><li><p>Each trunk divides into <strong>anterior and posterior divisions</strong> — six in total.</p></li><li><p>The divisions recombine into <strong>cords</strong> around the axillary artery, named</p></li></ul><p>for their position relative to its second part: anterior divisions of   superior and middle trunks form the <strong>lateral cord</strong>; all three posterior   divisions form the <strong>posterior cord</strong>; the anterior division of the inferior   trunk continues as the <strong>medial cord</strong>.</p><p>Note the exam favourite: the posterior cord receives fibres from <strong>all five roots</strong>, which is why every root value in the plexus contributes to extensor muscles.</p><h2>Branches Worth Memorizing</h2><p>Root and trunk branches are small but clinically famous:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Dorsal scapular nerve</strong> (C5) — rhomboids.</p></li><li><p><strong>Long thoracic nerve</strong> (C5–C7) — serratus anterior; injury causes winged</p></li></ul><p>scapula.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Suprascapular nerve</strong> (C5–C6, from the superior trunk) — supraspinatus</p></li></ul><p>and infraspinatus.</p><p>The cords give the terminal branches:</p><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Cord</p></th><th><p>Branches</p></th><th><p>Root value</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Lateral</p></td><td><p>Lateral pectoral nerve; musculocutaneous nerve; lateral root of median nerve</p></td><td><p>C5–C7</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Posterior</p></td><td><p>Upper and lower subscapular nerves; thoracodorsal nerve; axillary nerve; radial nerve</p></td><td><p>C5–T1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Medial</p></td><td><p>Medial pectoral nerve; medial cutaneous nerves of arm and forearm; ulnar nerve; medial root of median nerve</p></td><td><p>C8–T1 (ulnar may carry C7)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The median nerve is formed by lateral (C5–C7) and medial (C8–T1) roots joining on the front of the third part of the axillary artery — the two cords "shake hands" over it.</p><h2>Injuries: The Table Examiners Ask For</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Injury</p></th><th><p>Site / roots</p></th><th><p>Motor loss</p></th><th><p>Deformity or sign</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Erb''s palsy</p></td><td><p>Upper trunk C5–C6</p></td><td><p>Deltoid, biceps, supraspinatus, wrist extensors</p></td><td><p>Waiter''s tip: adducted, internally rotated arm, extended elbow, pronated forearm, flexed wrist</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Klumpke''s palsy</p></td><td><p>Lower trunk C8–T1</p></td><td><p>Intrinsic hand muscles, long flexors</p></td><td><p>Claw hand; ± Horner syndrome</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Radial nerve at spiral groove</p></td><td><p>Radial nerve, mid-shaft humerus</p></td><td><p>Wrist and finger extensors (triceps spared)</p></td><td><p>Wrist drop; sensory loss posterior forearm, dorsum of first web space</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Radial nerve in axilla</p></td><td><p>Radial nerve, axilla</p></td><td><p>All radial muscles including triceps</p></td><td><p>Wrist drop + loss of elbow extension (crutch pressure)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Axillary nerve at surgical neck</p></td><td><p>Axillary nerve</p></td><td><p>Deltoid, teres minor</p></td><td><p>Loss of shoulder abduction from 15–90°; numb regimental badge area</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Long thoracic nerve</p></td><td><p>C5–C7 on chest wall</p></td><td><p>Serratus anterior</p></td><td><p>Winged scapula</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Worked exam framing</h3><p>A vignette asks: <em>a newborn after shoulder dystocia holds the arm adducted and internally rotated with a flexed wrist — where is the lesion?</em> That is the waiter''s tip posture, so the answer is the <strong>upper trunk (C5–C6), Erb''s palsy</strong>. Change the vignette to a fall on the outstretched hand with inability to abduct beyond 15° and numbness over the deltoid, and the answer becomes <strong>axillary nerve at the surgical neck of the humerus</strong>. Examiners rarely leave the table above — they only re-dress it.</p><h2>Clinical Correlations</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Birth injury:</strong> excessive lateral flexion of the neck during delivery</p></li></ul><p>stretches the upper trunk (shoulder dystocia) or tears the lower trunk   (breech delivery pulling the arms). Prognosis of Erb''s palsy is generally   better than Klumpke''s.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Thoracic outlet syndrome:</strong> a cervical rib or tight scalene band</p></li></ul><p>compresses the inferior trunk (C8–T1) plus the subclavian artery — pain,   numbness along the ulnar border, and a positive Adson''s test.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Saturday night palsy:</strong> pressure on the arm against a bench compresses</p></li></ul><p>the radial nerve in the spiral groove — the classic cause of painless   wrist drop.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Post-mastectomy weakness:</strong> injury to the long thoracic nerve during</p></li></ul><p>breast surgery is the standard clinical source of winged scapula.</p><h2>Revision Pointers</h2><p>Draw the plexus from memory before every test — the sequence <em>roots → trunks → divisions → cords → branches</em> is worth more marks than any single fact. Then test yourself the forward way (name the branches of the posterior cord) and the backward way (a claw hand with Horner syndrome means which roots?). Finish with the <a href="/practice/quiz/anatomy/" rel="noopener">anatomy practice quiz</a> and, for the physiology side of limb nerve function, the <a href="/notes/cardiac-cycle/" rel="noopener">cardiac cycle study note</a> is a reminder of how the same first-professional pattern of table-plus-vignette questions works in other subjects.</p><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li><p>Trace the formation of the brachial plexus from roots to cords and branches</p></li><li><p>Name the branches arising from each cord and their root values</p></li><li><p>Correlate upper and lower trunk injuries with their clinical deformities</p></li><li><p>Localize radial, axillary and long thoracic nerve injuries from clinical findings</p></li><li><p>Explain why birth trauma and shoulder dystocia produce specific plexus lesions</p></li><li><p>Apply the plexus map to short-case and MCQ questions on upper limb nerve testing</p></li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Roots</strong> — The anterior primary rami of C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1 that unite to form the plexus.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Erb''s palsy</strong> — Injury of the upper trunk (C5–C6) producing the waiter''s tip deformity — adducted, internally rotated arm with extended elbow and pronated forearm.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Klumpke''s palsy</strong> — Injury of the lower trunk (C8–T1) producing a claw hand, sometimes with Horner syndrome if T1 sympathetic fibres are involved.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Cords</strong> — Lateral, posterior and medial cords of the plexus, named for their position relative to the second part of the axillary artery.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Wrist drop</strong> — Loss of wrist extension from radial nerve injury, usually at the spiral groove of the humerus.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Winged scapula</strong> — Paralysis of serratus anterior from long thoracic nerve injury (C5–C7); the medial scapular border lifts off the chest wall on pushing.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Horner syndrome</strong> — Ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis and enophthalmos from interruption of T1 sympathetic fibres to the head, seen in some lower trunk lesions.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Regimental badge area</strong> — The skin over the lower deltoid supplied by the axillary nerve; loss of sensation here localizes an axillary nerve injury.</p></aside><h2>Important points</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-important" data-type="important"><p class="msn-block-label">Important</p><ul><li><p>Roots C5–T1 form three trunks, six divisions, three cords and five main terminal branches.</p></li><li><p>The posterior cord carries fibres from all five roots — every root of the plexus supplies extensor compartments.</p></li><li><p>Erb''s palsy is an upper trunk lesion (C5–C6); Klumpke''s is a lower trunk lesion (C8–T1).</p></li><li><p>Radial nerve injury at the spiral groove spares triceps but causes wrist drop.</p></li><li><p>Axillary nerve injury at the surgical neck of the humerus paralyses deltoid and causes loss of sensation over the regimental badge area.</p></li><li><p>Long thoracic nerve injury causes winged scapula; it arises from the roots, not from a cord.</p></li><li><p>Musculocutaneous nerve pierces coracobrachialis; median nerve has no muscles supplied in the arm.</p></li></ul></aside><h2>Key formulas</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p><strong>Brachial plexus = 5 roots → 3 trunks → 6 divisions → 3 cords → branches</strong> — The branching sequence students must reproduce from memory in one line.</p></aside><h2>Summary</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p>The brachial plexus is formed by the anterior primary rami of C5 to T1, which unite into superior, middle and inferior trunks, each dividing into anterior and posterior divisions that recombine into lateral, posterior and medial cords. The cords give the five terminal branches — musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median and ulnar — and the pattern of injury maps onto this architecture: upper trunk lesions produce Erb''s waiter''s tip deformity, lower trunk lesions produce Klumpke''s claw hand, and individual nerves fail at predictable bony points such as the spiral groove and surgical neck of the humerus.</p></aside><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which nerve roots are involved in Erb''s palsy and what is the deformity?</h3><p>C5 and C6, the upper trunk. The arm hangs adducted and internally rotated, the elbow is extended, the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed — the waiter''s tip posture. It classically follows shoulder dystocia in delivery or a blow to the shoulder.</p><h3>Why does Klumpke''s palsy sometimes include Horner syndrome?</h3><p>The lower trunk carries T1 fibres, and T1 also contributes to the sympathetic supply of the head through the stellate ganglion. If the lesion catches these fibres the patient develops ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis on the same side, alongside the claw hand.</p><h3>Why is triceps spared when the radial nerve is damaged in the spiral groove?</h3><p>Branches to triceps leave the radial nerve before it enters the spiral groove on the posterior humerus. A lesion in the groove therefore causes wrist drop and sensory loss over the posterior forearm, but elbow extension is preserved.</p><h3>What is the clinical test for long thoracic nerve injury?</h3><p>Ask the patient to push against a wall with the arm forward. Serratus anterior paralysis lets the medial border of the scapula lift off the thorax — winged scapula. The nerve (C5–C7) is vulnerable in chest wall surgery and radical mastectomy.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Gray''s Anatomy for Students. Elsevier</li><li>Moore''s Clinically Oriented Anatomy. Wolters Kluwer</li><li>Snell''s Clinical Anatomy by Regions. Wolters Kluwer</li></ol></aside>',
