INSERT INTO articles (
  title, slug, excerpt, content_json, content_html, article_type, status, subject_id, topic_id, author_id, published_at, seo_title, meta_description, robots, reading_minutes, created_at, updated_at
) VALUES (
  'Cardiac Cycle: Complete Study Notes',
  'cardiac-cycle',
  'Cardiac cycle study notes covering all seven phases with pressures, valve positions and volumes, heart sounds, the pressure-volume loop, and cardiac output and ejection fraction worked examples.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These notes walk through the cardiac cycle phase by phase — what the pressures are doing, which valves are open, and where the volume sits — then tie the phases to heart sounds, the pressure-volume loop, and the cardiac output calculations exams always ask."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cardiac cycle is the heart''s repeating unit of work: fill, contract, empty, relax. Physiology exams test it three ways — a table of phases, the timing of heart sounds, and a calculation of cardiac output or ejection fraction. All three rest on one principle: "},{"type":"text","text":"valves open and close passively in response to pressure gradients","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", never by their own effort. Keep that in mind and every phase becomes deducible rather than memorizable."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Duration and Divisions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At a heart rate of 75 beats per minute the cycle lasts "},{"type":"text","text":"0.8 seconds","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": systole about 0.3 s and diastole about 0.5 s. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens proportionally more than systole — which is why very fast rates compromise filling and coronary perfusion, since the coronaries fill during diastole."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Seven Phases"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Phase"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial pressure vs ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ventricular pressure"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Valves"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ventricular volume"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Atrial systole"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial > ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising slightly"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AV open, semilunar closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising to EDV (~120 mL)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Isovolumetric contraction"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial < ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising steeply"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unchanged (EDV)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Rapid ejection"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial < ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising to peak (~120 mmHg)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Semilunar open, AV closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Falling fast"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Reduced ejection"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Falling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Falling slowly from peak"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Semilunar open, AV closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Falling slowly to ~50 mL"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Isovolumetric relaxation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial rising, ventricular falling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Falling steeply"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unchanged (ESV)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Rapid filling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial > ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Near zero"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AV open, semilunar closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising fast"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Diastasis (slow filling)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial ≈ ventricular"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Near zero"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AV open, semilunar closed"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rising slowly"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two details earn marks: the "},{"type":"text","text":"ventricle never empties","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — about 50 mL remains as end-systolic volume — and "},{"type":"text","text":"most filling occurs in phase 6","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", not during atrial systole, which only tops the ventricle up by 20–30 percent (the atrial kick)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading the pressure traces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","text":"aortic pressure trace","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows the "},{"type":"text","text":"dicrotic notch","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" when the aortic valve closes, and the aortic pressure never falls to zero because elastic recoil of the large arteries sustains it. The "},{"type":"text","text":"left atrial (or venous pulse) trace","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows the "},{"type":"text","text":"a, c and v waves","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"type":"text","text":"a","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from atrial contraction, "},{"type":"text","text":"c","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from bulging of AV valve leaflets during isovolumetric contraction, and "},{"type":"text","text":"v","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from venous filling of the atrium while the AV valve is shut. The "},{"type":"text","text":"x descent","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" follows atrial relaxation, the "},{"type":"text","text":"y descent","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" the opening of the AV valve."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Heart Sounds"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S1","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — closure of mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"isovolumetric contraction; coincides with the R wave of the ECG."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S2","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — closure of aortic and pulmonary valves at the start of"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"isovolumetric relaxation; its splitting widens on inspiration because   increased venous return delays pulmonary valve closure."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S3","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — rapid ventricular filling in early diastole; normal in youth,"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"suspicious of failure in older adults."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S4","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, just before S1;"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"absent in atrial fibrillation because the atria do not contract."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Pressure-Volume Loop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Draw volume on the x-axis and pressure on the y-axis for the left ventricle:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Filling","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the bottom limb moving right from ESV (~50 mL) to EDV"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(~120 mL) at near-zero pressure."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Isovolumetric contraction","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the vertical rise from EDV at constant"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"volume."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ejection","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the top curving limb from the aortic valve opening"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"pressure to peak systolic pressure (~120 mmHg), then back down to ESV."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Isovolumetric relaxation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the vertical drop from ESV to the filling"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"line."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The width of the loop is the stroke volume; the area inside the loop is the external stroke work. Aortic stenosis makes the loop taller and narrower; volume overload makes it wider; heart failure shifts it rightward."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Calculations: The Worked Example Exams Use"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A patient has an end-diastolic volume of 120 mL, an end-systolic volume of 50 mL, and a heart rate of 72 beats per minute."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stroke volume","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" = EDV − ESV = 120 − 50 = 70 mL."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cardiac output","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" = SV × HR = 70 × 72 = 5040 mL/min ≈ 5 L/min."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ejection fraction","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" = 70 ÷ 120 × 100 ≈ 58 percent."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the exam twist: the same patient develops a cardiomyopathy, EDV rises to 200 mL and stroke volume falls to 60 mL — ejection fraction drops to 30 percent, defining significant systolic dysfunction even though stroke volume has fallen only modestly. This is exactly why ejection fraction, not stroke volume, is the number cardiologists quote."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinical Framing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A short-case viva will point at the phase table and ask \"which murmur occurs here?\" Systolic murmurs occupy the interval between S1 and S2 (ejection phases), diastolic murmurs the interval after S2 (filling phases). A patient in atrial fibrillation loses the atrial kick and the "},{"type":"text","text":"a","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" wave; if their ventricle is also stiff, filling suffers twice — the physiological reason such patients deteriorate when the rate runs fast and diastasis shortens."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revise alongside the "},{"type":"text","text":"glycolysis study note","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/notes/glycolysis/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the biochemistry of myocardial energy supply, and test yourself with the "},{"type":"text","text":"physiology practice quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/physiology/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Learning objectives"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List the phases of the cardiac cycle in order with their durations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"State the pressure, valve position and ventricular volume for each phase"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Relate each heart sound to the event that produces it"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Draw and label a left ventricular pressure-volume loop"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Calculate cardiac output and ejection fraction from given values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explain why the atrial contribution to filling matters in ventricular disease"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key terms"}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Isovolumetric contraction","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The phase after the QRS when ventricular pressure rises with all four valves closed; the ventricle contracts without changing volume."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Isovolumetric relaxation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The phase after semilunar valve closure when ventricular pressure falls with all valves closed until the AV valves open."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stroke volume","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — End-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume; the blood ejected per beat, about 70 mL at rest."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ejection fraction","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, expressed as a percentage; normally about 55–70 percent."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"dicrotic notch","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The brief pressure rise in the aortic trace when the aortic valve snaps shut, caused by elastic recoil of the aorta."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Diastasis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The slow-filling phase of mid to late diastasis, the longest phase of the cycle, when most ventricular filling has already occurred."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atrial kick","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The 20 to 30 percent of ventricular filling contributed by atrial systole; lost in atrial fibrillation."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"End-diastolic volume","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The volume of blood in the ventricle at the end of filling, normally about 120 mL."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Important points"}]},{"type":"important","content":[{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At 75 beats per minute the cycle lasts 0.8 seconds — systole 0.3 s and diastole 0.5 s."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four valves are closed during isovolumetric contraction and isovolumetric relaxation; never simultaneously at any other time."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S1 marks closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves; S2 marks closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Maximum ventricular pressure is reached during the ejection phase; maximum volume during atrial systole."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most ventricular filling occurs in early diastole, not during atrial systole."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"S3 is normal in children and young adults; S4 indicates a stiff ventricle and is always abnormal in adults."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The atria and ventricles never contract at the same time — atrial systole finishes just before ventricular systole begins."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key formulas"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cardiac output = Stroke volume × Heart rate","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The volume of blood pumped by each ventricle per minute; at rest about 70 mL × 72 = roughly 5 L/min."}]}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ejection fraction = (Stroke volume ÷ End-diastolic volume) × 100","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The fraction of end-diastolic volume ejected per beat; below about 40 percent indicates ventricular dysfunction."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One cardiac cycle equals one systole and one diastole, about 0.8 seconds at 75 beats per minute. Systole runs from isovolumetric contraction through rapid and reduced ejection; diastole runs from isovolumetric relaxation through rapid filling, diastasis and atrial systole. Valve positions are decided purely by pressure gradients, the first and second heart sounds mark AV and semilunar valve closure respectively, and the pressure-volume loop closes each cycle with a stroke volume that multiplies by heart rate to give the cardiac output."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which phase of the cardiac cycle has the highest ventricular pressure?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ejection phase, specifically just before the aortic valve closes — peak systolic pressure, around 120 mmHg in the left ventricle. Isovolumetric contraction is the phase of steepest pressure rise, but the maximum itself is reached during ejection."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When are all four cardiac valves closed?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"During the two isovolumetric phases — contraction, while ventricular pressure climbs above atrial but below aortic pressure, and relaxation, while it falls between the same two thresholds. At every other point at least one valve pair is open."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What produces the third heart sound?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rapid, turbulent filling of the ventricle in early diastole. It is normal in children, young adults and pregnancy; in older adults it suggests volume overload or ventricular failure. S4, by contrast, comes from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"If stroke volume is 70 mL and heart rate is 72 per minute, what is the cardiac output?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cardiac output = 70 × 72 = 5040 mL per minute, about 5 litres. If the end-diastolic volume is 120 mL, the ejection fraction is 70/120 — roughly 58 percent, comfortably in the normal range."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","source":"Elsevier"},{"title":"Ganong''s Review of Medical Physiology","source":"McGraw Hill"}]}}]}',
  '<h2>Overview</h2><p>These notes walk through the cardiac cycle phase by phase — what the pressures are doing, which valves are open, and where the volume sits — then tie the phases to heart sounds, the pressure-volume loop, and the cardiac output calculations exams always ask.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>The cardiac cycle is the heart''s repeating unit of work: fill, contract, empty, relax. Physiology exams test it three ways — a table of phases, the timing of heart sounds, and a calculation of cardiac output or ejection fraction. All three rest on one principle: <strong>valves open and close passively in response to pressure gradients</strong>, never by their own effort. Keep that in mind and every phase becomes deducible rather than memorizable.</p><h2>Duration and Divisions</h2><p>At a heart rate of 75 beats per minute the cycle lasts <strong>0.8 seconds</strong>: systole about 0.3 s and diastole about 0.5 s. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens proportionally more than systole — which is why very fast rates compromise filling and coronary perfusion, since the coronaries fill during diastole.</p><h2>The Seven Phases</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Phase</p></th><th><p>Atrial pressure vs ventricular</p></th><th><p>Ventricular pressure</p></th><th><p>Valves</p></th><th><p>Ventricular volume</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>1. Atrial systole</p></td><td><p>Atrial &gt; ventricular</p></td><td><p>Rising slightly</p></td><td><p>AV open, semilunar closed</p></td><td><p>Rising to EDV (~120 mL)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>2. Isovolumetric contraction</p></td><td><p>Atrial &lt; ventricular</p></td><td><p>Rising steeply</p></td><td><p>All closed</p></td><td><p>Unchanged (EDV)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>3. Rapid ejection</p></td><td><p>Atrial &lt; ventricular</p></td><td><p>Rising to peak (~120 mmHg)</p></td><td><p>Semilunar open, AV closed</p></td><td><p>Falling fast</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>4. Reduced ejection</p></td><td><p>Falling</p></td><td><p>Falling slowly from peak</p></td><td><p>Semilunar open, AV closed</p></td><td><p>Falling slowly to ~50 mL</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>5. Isovolumetric relaxation</p></td><td><p>Atrial rising, ventricular falling</p></td><td><p>Falling steeply</p></td><td><p>All closed</p></td><td><p>Unchanged (ESV)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>6. Rapid filling</p></td><td><p>Atrial &gt; ventricular</p></td><td><p>Near zero</p></td><td><p>AV open, semilunar closed</p></td><td><p>Rising fast</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>7. Diastasis (slow filling)</p></td><td><p>Atrial ≈ ventricular</p></td><td><p>Near zero</p></td><td><p>AV open, semilunar closed</p></td><td><p>Rising slowly</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two details earn marks: the <strong>ventricle never empties</strong> — about 50 mL remains as end-systolic volume — and <strong>most filling occurs in phase 6</strong>, not during atrial systole, which only tops the ventricle up by 20–30 percent (the atrial kick).</p><h3>Reading the pressure traces</h3><p>The <strong>aortic pressure trace</strong> shows the <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> when the aortic valve closes, and the aortic pressure never falls to zero because elastic recoil of the large arteries sustains it. The <strong>left atrial (or venous pulse) trace</strong> shows the <strong>a, c and v waves</strong>: <em>a</em> from atrial contraction, <em>c</em> from bulging of AV valve leaflets during isovolumetric contraction, and <em>v</em> from venous filling of the atrium while the AV valve is shut. The <em>x descent</em> follows atrial relaxation, the <em>y descent</em> the opening of the AV valve.</p><h2>Heart Sounds</h2><ul><li><p><strong>S1</strong> — closure of mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of</p></li></ul><p>isovolumetric contraction; coincides with the R wave of the ECG.</p><ul><li><p><strong>S2</strong> — closure of aortic and pulmonary valves at the start of</p></li></ul><p>isovolumetric relaxation; its splitting widens on inspiration because   increased venous return delays pulmonary valve closure.</p><ul><li><p><strong>S3</strong> — rapid ventricular filling in early diastole; normal in youth,</p></li></ul><p>suspicious of failure in older adults.</p><ul><li><p><strong>S4</strong> — atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, just before S1;</p></li></ul><p>absent in atrial fibrillation because the atria do not contract.</p><h2>The Pressure-Volume Loop</h2><p>Draw volume on the x-axis and pressure on the y-axis for the left ventricle:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Filling</strong> — the bottom limb moving right from ESV (~50 mL) to EDV</p></li></ol><p>(~120 mL) at near-zero pressure.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Isovolumetric contraction</strong> — the vertical rise from EDV at constant</p></li></ol><p>volume.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Ejection</strong> — the top curving limb from the aortic valve opening</p></li></ol><p>pressure to peak systolic pressure (~120 mmHg), then back down to ESV.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation</strong> — the vertical drop from ESV to the filling</p></li></ol><p>line.</p><p>The width of the loop is the stroke volume; the area inside the loop is the external stroke work. Aortic stenosis makes the loop taller and narrower; volume overload makes it wider; heart failure shifts it rightward.</p><h2>Calculations: The Worked Example Exams Use</h2><p>A patient has an end-diastolic volume of 120 mL, an end-systolic volume of 50 mL, and a heart rate of 72 beats per minute.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Stroke volume</strong> = EDV − ESV = 120 − 50 = 70 mL.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cardiac output</strong> = SV × HR = 70 × 72 = 5040 mL/min ≈ 5 L/min.</p></li><li><p><strong>Ejection fraction</strong> = 70 ÷ 120 × 100 ≈ 58 percent.</p></li></ul><p>Now the exam twist: the same patient develops a cardiomyopathy, EDV rises to 200 mL and stroke volume falls to 60 mL — ejection fraction drops to 30 percent, defining significant systolic dysfunction even though stroke volume has fallen only modestly. This is exactly why ejection fraction, not stroke volume, is the number cardiologists quote.</p><h2>Clinical Framing</h2><p>A short-case viva will point at the phase table and ask "which murmur occurs here?" Systolic murmurs occupy the interval between S1 and S2 (ejection phases), diastolic murmurs the interval after S2 (filling phases). A patient in atrial fibrillation loses the atrial kick and the <em>a</em> wave; if their ventricle is also stiff, filling suffers twice — the physiological reason such patients deteriorate when the rate runs fast and diastasis shortens.</p><p>Revise alongside the <a href="/notes/glycolysis/" rel="noopener">glycolysis study note</a> for the biochemistry of myocardial energy supply, and test yourself with the <a href="/practice/quiz/physiology/" rel="noopener">physiology practice quiz</a>.</p><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li><p>List the phases of the cardiac cycle in order with their durations</p></li><li><p>State the pressure, valve position and ventricular volume for each phase</p></li><li><p>Relate each heart sound to the event that produces it</p></li><li><p>Draw and label a left ventricular pressure-volume loop</p></li><li><p>Calculate cardiac output and ejection fraction from given values</p></li><li><p>Explain why the atrial contribution to filling matters in ventricular disease</p></li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Isovolumetric contraction</strong> — The phase after the QRS when ventricular pressure rises with all four valves closed; the ventricle contracts without changing volume.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation</strong> — The phase after semilunar valve closure when ventricular pressure falls with all valves closed until the AV valves open.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Stroke volume</strong> — End-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume; the blood ejected per beat, about 70 mL at rest.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Ejection fraction</strong> — Stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume, expressed as a percentage; normally about 55–70 percent.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>dicrotic notch</strong> — The brief pressure rise in the aortic trace when the aortic valve snaps shut, caused by elastic recoil of the aorta.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Diastasis</strong> — The slow-filling phase of mid to late diastasis, the longest phase of the cycle, when most ventricular filling has already occurred.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Atrial kick</strong> — The 20 to 30 percent of ventricular filling contributed by atrial systole; lost in atrial fibrillation.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>End-diastolic volume</strong> — The volume of blood in the ventricle at the end of filling, normally about 120 mL.</p></aside><h2>Important points</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-important" data-type="important"><p class="msn-block-label">Important</p><ul><li><p>At 75 beats per minute the cycle lasts 0.8 seconds — systole 0.3 s and diastole 0.5 s.</p></li><li><p>All four valves are closed during isovolumetric contraction and isovolumetric relaxation; never simultaneously at any other time.</p></li><li><p>S1 marks closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves; S2 marks closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves.</p></li><li><p>Maximum ventricular pressure is reached during the ejection phase; maximum volume during atrial systole.</p></li><li><p>Most ventricular filling occurs in early diastole, not during atrial systole.</p></li><li><p>S3 is normal in children and young adults; S4 indicates a stiff ventricle and is always abnormal in adults.</p></li><li><p>The atria and ventricles never contract at the same time — atrial systole finishes just before ventricular systole begins.</p></li></ul></aside><h2>Key formulas</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p><strong>Cardiac output = Stroke volume × Heart rate</strong> — The volume of blood pumped by each ventricle per minute; at rest about 70 mL × 72 = roughly 5 L/min.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p><strong>Ejection fraction = (Stroke volume ÷ End-diastolic volume) × 100</strong> — The fraction of end-diastolic volume ejected per beat; below about 40 percent indicates ventricular dysfunction.</p></aside><h2>Summary</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p>One cardiac cycle equals one systole and one diastole, about 0.8 seconds at 75 beats per minute. Systole runs from isovolumetric contraction through rapid and reduced ejection; diastole runs from isovolumetric relaxation through rapid filling, diastasis and atrial systole. Valve positions are decided purely by pressure gradients, the first and second heart sounds mark AV and semilunar valve closure respectively, and the pressure-volume loop closes each cycle with a stroke volume that multiplies by heart rate to give the cardiac output.</p></aside><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which phase of the cardiac cycle has the highest ventricular pressure?</h3><p>The ejection phase, specifically just before the aortic valve closes — peak systolic pressure, around 120 mmHg in the left ventricle. Isovolumetric contraction is the phase of steepest pressure rise, but the maximum itself is reached during ejection.</p><h3>When are all four cardiac valves closed?</h3><p>During the two isovolumetric phases — contraction, while ventricular pressure climbs above atrial but below aortic pressure, and relaxation, while it falls between the same two thresholds. At every other point at least one valve pair is open.</p><h3>What produces the third heart sound?</h3><p>Rapid, turbulent filling of the ventricle in early diastole. It is normal in children, young adults and pregnancy; in older adults it suggests volume overload or ventricular failure. S4, by contrast, comes from atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle.</p><h3>If stroke volume is 70 mL and heart rate is 72 per minute, what is the cardiac output?</h3><p>Cardiac output = 70 × 72 = 5040 mL per minute, about 5 litres. If the end-diastolic volume is 120 mL, the ejection fraction is 70/120 — roughly 58 percent, comfortably in the normal range.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology. Elsevier</li><li>Ganong''s Review of Medical Physiology. McGraw Hill</li></ol></aside>',
