) VALUES (
  'Cell Injury: Complete Study Notes',
  'cell-injury',
  'Cell injury notes covering causes, the mechanisms that decide reversible versus irreversible injury, the necrosis patterns, necrosis versus apoptosis, and the mediators of inflammation.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These notes explain what injures cells, the intracellular mechanisms — ATP depletion, calcium influx, free radicals, membrane damage — that tip reversible injury into necrosis, the morphology examiners expect, and the necrosis versus apoptosis comparison that anchors the topic."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything in pathology begins here: aetiology of disease, then mechanism, then morphology. Cell injury is the first chapter of that story. The examiner''s questions are predictable — what caused it, is it reversible, what does it look like, and which pattern of necrosis is this? The "},{"type":"text","text":"inflammation and repair topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/inflammation-and-repair/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" picks up exactly where this note ends, at the tissue''s response to death."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Causes of Cell Injury"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hypoxia and ischemia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the commonest cause: atherosclerotic"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"occlusion, thrombus, strangulation of hernia, anemia, carbon monoxide   poisoning. Ischemia injures more than hypoxia alone because glucose and   washout are lost too."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Physical agents","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — trauma, burns, frostbite, radiation, electric"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"shock, pressure."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Chemical agents and drugs","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — from alcohol and heavy metals to"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"therapeutic drugs in overdose; even oxygen at high concentration   (free-radical injury)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Infectious agents","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — viruses, bacteria including exotoxins and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"endotoxin, fungi, parasites."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Immunologic reactions","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — anaphylaxis, autoimmune disease, immune-complex"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"deposition."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Genetic derangements","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — from enzyme defects to structural protein"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"mutations."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nutritional imbalances","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — protein-energy malnutrition, vitamin"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"deficiencies, and obesity itself as a pro-inflammatory state."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mechanisms: The Pathogenesis That Decides Reversibility"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whatever the cause, injured cells converge on a few final pathways:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ATP depletion","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — failure of the sodium pump causes sodium and water"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"influx (cellular swelling) and of the calcium pump causes cytosolic    calcium accumulation."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mitochondrial damage","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — both victim and executioner: damaged"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"mitochondria release cytochrome c (triggering apoptosis) and generate    further free radicals."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Calcium influx","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — activates phospholipases, proteases, ATPases and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"endonucleases, which dismantle membranes, cytoskeleton and DNA."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reactive oxygen species","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — superoxide, hydroxyl radical and hydrogen"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"peroxide attack lipids (lipid peroxidation), proteins and DNA; central to    ischemia-reperfusion injury, inflammation and chemical toxicity."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Membrane damage","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the critical event. While plasma, mitochondrial and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"lysosomal membranes hold, the injury is reversible; once they fail,    enzymes leak, digestion begins, and the cell is irreversibly dead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","text":"reperfusion paradox","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is examinable: restoring flow after ischemia delivers oxygen that fuels new free radical bursts, and infiltrating neutrophils add their own — so injury can worsen just as treatment begins."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Morphology of Injury"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reversible:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cellular swelling","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (hydropic change, vacuolar degeneration) — pale,"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"swollen, heavy organs; microscopically clear cytoplasmic vacuoles."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fatty change","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — lipid vacuoles in parenchymal cells; classically in the"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"liver with alcohol, anoxia or toxins."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other reversible touches: eosinophilia of cytoplasm, chromatin clumping."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Irreversible — necrosis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cytoplasmic","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — increased eosinophilia, then myelin figures, and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ultimately calcification of dead cells."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nuclear","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"text","text":"pyknosis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (condensation), "},{"type":"text","text":"karyorrhexis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(fragmentation), "},{"type":"text","text":"karyolysis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (dissolution by DNase)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","text":"inflammatory response","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that follows is what distinguishes necrosis"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"histologically from apoptosis."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinically, dead cells release their enzymes: this is the basis of serum "},{"type":"text","text":"troponin","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" after myocardial infarction, "},{"type":"text","text":"ALT/AST","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in hepatocyte injury, and "},{"type":"text","text":"lipase","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in acute pancreatitis — morphology made measurable."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Six Patterns of Necrosis"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mechanism / appearance"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classic example"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coagulative"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Architecture preserved as firm ghost outline; denatured proteins block digestion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Myocardial, renal, splenic infarcts"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Liquefactive"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Enzymatic digestion to liquid"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brain infarct; abscess cavity"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Caseous"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amorphous granular debris, \"cheese-like\", ringed by granuloma"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tuberculosis"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fat"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipases release fatty acids that saponify; chalky white flecks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Acute pancreatitis; traumatic breast fat necrosis"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fibrinoid"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Immune complexes and plasma proteins deposited in vessel walls, bright pink"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Polyarteritis nodosa; malignant hypertension"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gangrenous"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coagulative necrosis of a limb, plus liquefactive if infected (wet gangrene)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Diabetic foot, strangulated hernia"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worked exam framing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A vignette describes a 55-year-old diabetic with a black, dry, mummified toe: that is "},{"type":"text","text":"dry gangrene","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — coagulative necrosis in a limb. Add foul smell, crepitus and a wet, putrid appearance, and it becomes "},{"type":"text","text":"wet gangrene","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", where bacterial liquefactive necrosis is superimposed. The same slide-based logic applies everywhere in pathology: name the pattern, then name the disease."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Necrosis versus Apoptosis"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Feature"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Necrosis"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apoptosis"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cause"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Injury, always pathologic"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Programmed; physiologic or pathologic"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cells affected"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contiguous groups"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Single, scattered cells"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cell size"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Swollen"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shrunken"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nucleus"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Condensation and fragmentation into nucleosome-sized bits"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Membrane"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ruptured"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intact, budding into apoptotic bodies"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ATP required"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inflammation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Absent"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Examples"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Infarct, toxin injury"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Embryologic remodelling, thymic involution; viral hepatitis councilman bodies, tumor regression"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The exam trap is "},{"type":"text","text":"apoptosis in disease","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": it is not always physiologic. Viral hepatitis kills hepatocytes largely by apoptosis (Councilman bodies), and failure of apoptosis contributes to cancer and autoimmunity — which is the conceptual bridge to neoplasia later in the course."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mediators of Inflammation: A Bridge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cell death summons inflammation, and the mediators are the next exam''s material. In outline:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Histamine","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" from mast cells — the fastest mediator of vasodilation and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"vascular permeability."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Arachidonic acid metabolites","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — prostaglandins (vasodilation, pain,"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"fever) and leukotrienes (bronchoconstriction, chemotaxis); the target of   NSAIDs."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Complement","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins, C5a chemotaxis, membrane"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"attack complex."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cytokines","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — interleukin-1 and tumor necrosis factor drive fever and"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"the acute phase response."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kinins","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — bradykinin, the mediator of pain and permeability."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The full sequence of hemodynamic and cellular events, chronic inflammation and repair continues in the "},{"type":"text","text":"inflammation and repair topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/inflammation-and-repair/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revision Pointers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Draw a two-column list — reversible versus irreversible — and pin each morphologic finding to its column. Then take the necrosis table and convert each row into a one-line vignette, because that is how it is asked. Finish with the "},{"type":"text","text":"pathology practice quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/pathology/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", and keep the "},{"type":"text","text":"glycolysis study note","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/notes/glycolysis/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" nearby: ATP depletion is the mechanism, glycolysis is the biochemistry, and ischemia is where the two subjects meet."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Learning objectives"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List the major causes of cell injury with clinical examples of each"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Describe the biochemical mechanisms that mediate cell injury"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Distinguish reversible from irreversible injury morphologically and mechanistically"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classify the six patterns of necrosis and give a classic clinical example of each"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contrast necrosis with apoptosis in mechanism, morphology and significance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outline the key chemical mediators of the acute inflammatory response"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key terms"}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hypoxia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Oxygen deprivation that forces cells to switch to anaerobic glycolysis, depleting ATP and causing lactic acid accumulation — the most common cause of cell injury."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ischemia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Loss of blood supply to a tissue, combining hypoxia with loss of nutrient delivery and metabolite removal, hence more injurious than hypoxia alone."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reversible injury","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Injury from which the cell recovers when the insult is removed; morphologically cellular swelling and fatty change."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Necrosis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Pathologic, always injurious cell death in living tissue, with enzymatic digestion, membrane rupture and an inflammatory response."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apoptosis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Programmed, energy-dependent cell death without membrane rupture or inflammation — physiologic (as in embryogenesis) or pathologic."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Caseous necrosis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Amorphous, cheese-like necrotic debris enclosed by a granulomatous reaction, characteristic of tuberculosis."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free radicals","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Highly reactive oxygen species with unpaired electrons that damage lipids, proteins and nucleic acids; injurious in ischemia-reperfusion and chemical injury."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Karyorrhexis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Fragmentation of a pyknotic nucleus into bits, one of the nuclear changes of necrosis along with pyknosis and karyolysis."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Important points"}]},{"type":"important","content":[{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ischemia is more damaging than pure hypoxia because it also cuts off glucose supply and waste removal, not just oxygen."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The two morphologic hallmarks of reversible injury are cellular swelling and fatty change; both reflect ATP-dependent pump failure and membrane damage."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The point of no return is severe membrane damage — mitochondrial, plasma membrane and lysosomal — releasing enzymes into the serum."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Serum enzyme patterns localize cell death — troponin for myocardium, ALT for hepatocytes, amylase and lipase for pancreas, creatine kinase for muscle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coagulative necrosis is the pattern of hypoxic death in all solid organs except the brain; the brain liquefies."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Granulomas with caseous necrosis are the signature of tuberculosis; fibrinoid necrosis signals immune-mediated vascular damage."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apoptosis needs ATP, produces no inflammation and leaves apoptotic bodies for phagocytosis; necrosis does neither."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cells are injured by hypoxia and ischemia above all, and by physical, chemical, infectious, immunologic, genetic and nutritional causes. The common intracellular pathways are ATP depletion, mitochondrial damage, calcium influx, reactive oxygen species and membrane failure; while membranes hold, injury is reversible — cellular swelling and fatty change — but once membranes rupture the cell dies by necrosis, whose six patterns (coagulative, liquefactive, caseous, fat, fibrinoid, gangrenous) each point to a specific clinical disease. Apoptosis contrasts at every step: programmed, energy-dependent, silent, and without inflammation, and the tissue response to death begins with the mediators of acute inflammation."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which features distinguish reversible from irreversible cell injury?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reversible injury shows cellular swelling, loss of microvilli, and fatty change, with clumped chromatin but an intact plasma membrane. Irreversibility is marked by extensive membrane damage, mitochondrial vacuolization, lysosomal rupture and nuclear changes — pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis. Functionally, the cell can no longer generate ATP or maintain its permeability barrier."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why does the brain undergo liquefactive rather than coagulative necrosis after infarction?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brain tissue has little connective tissue stroma to hold a coagulative architecture, and it is rich in lysosomal enzymes and lipids. The dead tissue therefore digests into a soft, liquid cavity that is eventually cleared by macrophages, leaving a glial scar."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Give one clinical example each of coagulative, caseous and fibrinoid necrosis."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coagulative — myocardial infarction or renal infarct. Caseous — the necrotic centre of a tuberculous granuloma. Fibrinoid — the vessel wall in malignant hypertension or immune vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How does apoptosis differ from necrosis in morphology and outcome?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apoptosis affects single cells that shrink, bud into apoptotic bodies and are phagocytosed without inflammation; it is ATP-dependent and can be physiologic. Necrosis affects contiguous cell groups that swell and rupture, releasing enzymes and provoking acute inflammation; it is always pathologic."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","source":"Elsevier"},{"title":"Kumar, Abbas, Aster: Robbins Basic Pathology","source":"Elsevier"}]}}]}',
  '<h2>Overview</h2><p>These notes explain what injures cells, the intracellular mechanisms — ATP depletion, calcium influx, free radicals, membrane damage — that tip reversible injury into necrosis, the morphology examiners expect, and the necrosis versus apoptosis comparison that anchors the topic.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Everything in pathology begins here: aetiology of disease, then mechanism, then morphology. Cell injury is the first chapter of that story. The examiner''s questions are predictable — what caused it, is it reversible, what does it look like, and which pattern of necrosis is this? The <a href="/topics/inflammation-and-repair/" rel="noopener">inflammation and repair topic hub</a> picks up exactly where this note ends, at the tissue''s response to death.</p><h2>Causes of Cell Injury</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Hypoxia and ischemia</strong> — the commonest cause: atherosclerotic</p></li></ul><p>occlusion, thrombus, strangulation of hernia, anemia, carbon monoxide   poisoning. Ischemia injures more than hypoxia alone because glucose and   washout are lost too.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Physical agents</strong> — trauma, burns, frostbite, radiation, electric</p></li></ul><p>shock, pressure.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Chemical agents and drugs</strong> — from alcohol and heavy metals to</p></li></ul><p>therapeutic drugs in overdose; even oxygen at high concentration   (free-radical injury).</p><ul><li><p><strong>Infectious agents</strong> — viruses, bacteria including exotoxins and</p></li></ul><p>endotoxin, fungi, parasites.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Immunologic reactions</strong> — anaphylaxis, autoimmune disease, immune-complex</p></li></ul><p>deposition.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Genetic derangements</strong> — from enzyme defects to structural protein</p></li></ul><p>mutations.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Nutritional imbalances</strong> — protein-energy malnutrition, vitamin</p></li></ul><p>deficiencies, and obesity itself as a pro-inflammatory state.</p><h2>Mechanisms: The Pathogenesis That Decides Reversibility</h2><p>Whatever the cause, injured cells converge on a few final pathways:</p><ol><li><p><strong>ATP depletion</strong> — failure of the sodium pump causes sodium and water</p></li></ol><p>influx (cellular swelling) and of the calcium pump causes cytosolic    calcium accumulation.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Mitochondrial damage</strong> — both victim and executioner: damaged</p></li></ol><p>mitochondria release cytochrome c (triggering apoptosis) and generate    further free radicals.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Calcium influx</strong> — activates phospholipases, proteases, ATPases and</p></li></ol><p>endonucleases, which dismantle membranes, cytoskeleton and DNA.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Reactive oxygen species</strong> — superoxide, hydroxyl radical and hydrogen</p></li></ol><p>peroxide attack lipids (lipid peroxidation), proteins and DNA; central to    ischemia-reperfusion injury, inflammation and chemical toxicity.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Membrane damage</strong> — the critical event. While plasma, mitochondrial and</p></li></ol><p>lysosomal membranes hold, the injury is reversible; once they fail,    enzymes leak, digestion begins, and the cell is irreversibly dead.</p><p>The <strong>reperfusion paradox</strong> is examinable: restoring flow after ischemia delivers oxygen that fuels new free radical bursts, and infiltrating neutrophils add their own — so injury can worsen just as treatment begins.</p><h2>Morphology of Injury</h2><p><strong>Reversible:</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>Cellular swelling</em> (hydropic change, vacuolar degeneration) — pale,</p></li></ul><p>swollen, heavy organs; microscopically clear cytoplasmic vacuoles.</p><ul><li><p><em>Fatty change</em> — lipid vacuoles in parenchymal cells; classically in the</p></li></ul><p>liver with alcohol, anoxia or toxins.</p><ul><li><p>Other reversible touches: eosinophilia of cytoplasm, chromatin clumping.</p></li></ul><p><strong>Irreversible — necrosis</strong> shows:</p><ul><li><p><em>Cytoplasmic</em> — increased eosinophilia, then myelin figures, and</p></li></ul><p>ultimately calcification of dead cells.</p><ul><li><p><em>Nuclear</em> — <strong>pyknosis</strong> (condensation), <strong>karyorrhexis</strong></p></li></ul><p>(fragmentation), <strong>karyolysis</strong> (dissolution by DNase).</p><ul><li><p>The <strong>inflammatory response</strong> that follows is what distinguishes necrosis</p></li></ul><p>histologically from apoptosis.</p><p>Clinically, dead cells release their enzymes: this is the basis of serum <strong>troponin</strong> after myocardial infarction, <strong>ALT/AST</strong> in hepatocyte injury, and <strong>lipase</strong> in acute pancreatitis — morphology made measurable.</p><h2>The Six Patterns of Necrosis</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Type</p></th><th><p>Mechanism / appearance</p></th><th><p>Classic example</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Coagulative</p></td><td><p>Architecture preserved as firm ghost outline; denatured proteins block digestion</p></td><td><p>Myocardial, renal, splenic infarcts</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Liquefactive</p></td><td><p>Enzymatic digestion to liquid</p></td><td><p>Brain infarct; abscess cavity</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Caseous</p></td><td><p>Amorphous granular debris, "cheese-like", ringed by granuloma</p></td><td><p>Tuberculosis</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Fat</p></td><td><p>Lipases release fatty acids that saponify; chalky white flecks</p></td><td><p>Acute pancreatitis; traumatic breast fat necrosis</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Fibrinoid</p></td><td><p>Immune complexes and plasma proteins deposited in vessel walls, bright pink</p></td><td><p>Polyarteritis nodosa; malignant hypertension</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Gangrenous</p></td><td><p>Coagulative necrosis of a limb, plus liquefactive if infected (wet gangrene)</p></td><td><p>Diabetic foot, strangulated hernia</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Worked exam framing</h3><p>A vignette describes a 55-year-old diabetic with a black, dry, mummified toe: that is <strong>dry gangrene</strong> — coagulative necrosis in a limb. Add foul smell, crepitus and a wet, putrid appearance, and it becomes <strong>wet gangrene</strong>, where bacterial liquefactive necrosis is superimposed. The same slide-based logic applies everywhere in pathology: name the pattern, then name the disease.</p><h2>Necrosis versus Apoptosis</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Feature</p></th><th><p>Necrosis</p></th><th><p>Apoptosis</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Cause</p></td><td><p>Injury, always pathologic</p></td><td><p>Programmed; physiologic or pathologic</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Cells affected</p></td><td><p>Contiguous groups</p></td><td><p>Single, scattered cells</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Cell size</p></td><td><p>Swollen</p></td><td><p>Shrunken</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Nucleus</p></td><td><p>Pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis</p></td><td><p>Condensation and fragmentation into nucleosome-sized bits</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Membrane</p></td><td><p>Ruptured</p></td><td><p>Intact, budding into apoptotic bodies</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>ATP required</p></td><td><p>No</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Inflammation</p></td><td><p>Present</p></td><td><p>Absent</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Examples</p></td><td><p>Infarct, toxin injury</p></td><td><p>Embryologic remodelling, thymic involution; viral hepatitis councilman bodies, tumor regression</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The exam trap is <strong>apoptosis in disease</strong>: it is not always physiologic. Viral hepatitis kills hepatocytes largely by apoptosis (Councilman bodies), and failure of apoptosis contributes to cancer and autoimmunity — which is the conceptual bridge to neoplasia later in the course.</p><h2>Mediators of Inflammation: A Bridge</h2><p>Cell death summons inflammation, and the mediators are the next exam''s material. In outline:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Histamine</strong> from mast cells — the fastest mediator of vasodilation and</p></li></ul><p>vascular permeability.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Arachidonic acid metabolites</strong> — prostaglandins (vasodilation, pain,</p></li></ul><p>fever) and leukotrienes (bronchoconstriction, chemotaxis); the target of   NSAIDs.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Complement</strong> — C3a and C5a anaphylatoxins, C5a chemotaxis, membrane</p></li></ul><p>attack complex.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Cytokines</strong> — interleukin-1 and tumor necrosis factor drive fever and</p></li></ul><p>the acute phase response.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Kinins</strong> — bradykinin, the mediator of pain and permeability.</p></li></ul><p>The full sequence of hemodynamic and cellular events, chronic inflammation and repair continues in the <a href="/topics/inflammation-and-repair/" rel="noopener">inflammation and repair topic hub</a>.</p><h2>Revision Pointers</h2><p>Draw a two-column list — reversible versus irreversible — and pin each morphologic finding to its column. Then take the necrosis table and convert each row into a one-line vignette, because that is how it is asked. Finish with the <a href="/practice/quiz/pathology/" rel="noopener">pathology practice quiz</a>, and keep the <a href="/notes/glycolysis/" rel="noopener">glycolysis study note</a> nearby: ATP depletion is the mechanism, glycolysis is the biochemistry, and ischemia is where the two subjects meet.</p><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li><p>List the major causes of cell injury with clinical examples of each</p></li><li><p>Describe the biochemical mechanisms that mediate cell injury</p></li><li><p>Distinguish reversible from irreversible injury morphologically and mechanistically</p></li><li><p>Classify the six patterns of necrosis and give a classic clinical example of each</p></li><li><p>Contrast necrosis with apoptosis in mechanism, morphology and significance</p></li><li><p>Outline the key chemical mediators of the acute inflammatory response</p></li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Hypoxia</strong> — Oxygen deprivation that forces cells to switch to anaerobic glycolysis, depleting ATP and causing lactic acid accumulation — the most common cause of cell injury.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Ischemia</strong> — Loss of blood supply to a tissue, combining hypoxia with loss of nutrient delivery and metabolite removal, hence more injurious than hypoxia alone.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Reversible injury</strong> — Injury from which the cell recovers when the insult is removed; morphologically cellular swelling and fatty change.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Necrosis</strong> — Pathologic, always injurious cell death in living tissue, with enzymatic digestion, membrane rupture and an inflammatory response.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Apoptosis</strong> — Programmed, energy-dependent cell death without membrane rupture or inflammation — physiologic (as in embryogenesis) or pathologic.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Caseous necrosis</strong> — Amorphous, cheese-like necrotic debris enclosed by a granulomatous reaction, characteristic of tuberculosis.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Free radicals</strong> — Highly reactive oxygen species with unpaired electrons that damage lipids, proteins and nucleic acids; injurious in ischemia-reperfusion and chemical injury.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Karyorrhexis</strong> — Fragmentation of a pyknotic nucleus into bits, one of the nuclear changes of necrosis along with pyknosis and karyolysis.</p></aside><h2>Important points</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-important" data-type="important"><p class="msn-block-label">Important</p><ul><li><p>Ischemia is more damaging than pure hypoxia because it also cuts off glucose supply and waste removal, not just oxygen.</p></li><li><p>The two morphologic hallmarks of reversible injury are cellular swelling and fatty change; both reflect ATP-dependent pump failure and membrane damage.</p></li><li><p>The point of no return is severe membrane damage — mitochondrial, plasma membrane and lysosomal — releasing enzymes into the serum.</p></li><li><p>Serum enzyme patterns localize cell death — troponin for myocardium, ALT for hepatocytes, amylase and lipase for pancreas, creatine kinase for muscle.</p></li><li><p>Coagulative necrosis is the pattern of hypoxic death in all solid organs except the brain; the brain liquefies.</p></li><li><p>Granulomas with caseous necrosis are the signature of tuberculosis; fibrinoid necrosis signals immune-mediated vascular damage.</p></li><li><p>Apoptosis needs ATP, produces no inflammation and leaves apoptotic bodies for phagocytosis; necrosis does neither.</p></li></ul></aside><h2>Summary</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p>Cells are injured by hypoxia and ischemia above all, and by physical, chemical, infectious, immunologic, genetic and nutritional causes. The common intracellular pathways are ATP depletion, mitochondrial damage, calcium influx, reactive oxygen species and membrane failure; while membranes hold, injury is reversible — cellular swelling and fatty change — but once membranes rupture the cell dies by necrosis, whose six patterns (coagulative, liquefactive, caseous, fat, fibrinoid, gangrenous) each point to a specific clinical disease. Apoptosis contrasts at every step: programmed, energy-dependent, silent, and without inflammation, and the tissue response to death begins with the mediators of acute inflammation.</p></aside><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which features distinguish reversible from irreversible cell injury?</h3><p>Reversible injury shows cellular swelling, loss of microvilli, and fatty change, with clumped chromatin but an intact plasma membrane. Irreversibility is marked by extensive membrane damage, mitochondrial vacuolization, lysosomal rupture and nuclear changes — pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis. Functionally, the cell can no longer generate ATP or maintain its permeability barrier.</p><h3>Why does the brain undergo liquefactive rather than coagulative necrosis after infarction?</h3><p>Brain tissue has little connective tissue stroma to hold a coagulative architecture, and it is rich in lysosomal enzymes and lipids. The dead tissue therefore digests into a soft, liquid cavity that is eventually cleared by macrophages, leaving a glial scar.</p><h3>Give one clinical example each of coagulative, caseous and fibrinoid necrosis.</h3><p>Coagulative — myocardial infarction or renal infarct. Caseous — the necrotic centre of a tuberculous granuloma. Fibrinoid — the vessel wall in malignant hypertension or immune vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa.</p><h3>How does apoptosis differ from necrosis in morphology and outcome?</h3><p>Apoptosis affects single cells that shrink, bud into apoptotic bodies and are phagocytosed without inflammation; it is ATP-dependent and can be physiologic. Necrosis affects contiguous cell groups that swell and rupture, releasing enzymes and provoking acute inflammation; it is always pathologic.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Robbins &amp; Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease. Elsevier</li><li>Kumar, Abbas, Aster: Robbins Basic Pathology. Elsevier</li></ol></aside>',
