) VALUES (
  'Gram-Positive vs Gram-Negative Bacteria: Complete Study Notes',
  'gram-positive-vs-gram-negative',
  'Notes comparing gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria: cell wall structure, Gram stain chemistry, clinically important genera, treatment implications, and MRSA and VRE.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Overview"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These notes contrast the gram-positive and gram-negative cell wall, explain the chemistry behind Gram staining, list the medically important genera on each side, and draw out the treatment and resistance implications — endotoxin, beta-lactamases, MRSA and VRE."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Gram stain is the oldest and still the fastest bench test in bacteriology: a smear from a colony or a specimen, four reagents, and within minutes you know which of two cell wall architectures you are facing — which shortens the antibiotic list before any culture grows. These notes cover the stain, the walls that explain it, the genera that matter, and the resistance story that continues in the "},{"type":"text","text":"antimicrobial resistance topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/antimicrobial-resistance/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Gram Stain and Why It Works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four steps, and the chemistry is the exam answer:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Crystal violet","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the primary stain; all cells take it up."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram''s iodine","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — a mordant that forms the large crystal violet-iodine"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"complex inside the cell."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alcohol-acetone decolorizer","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the decisive step. In gram-positive"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cells the thick peptidoglycan dehydrates, its pores shrink, and the    complex is trapped. In gram-negative cells the thin wall plus the    dissolving outer membrane lets the complex wash out."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Safranin","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the pink counterstain that colors the now-colorless"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"gram-negative cells."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive stain purple; gram-negative stain pink.","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The staining difference is wall architecture, not membrane chemistry of the dye itself — a distinction examiners like to press."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cell Wall Architecture"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Structure"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-negative"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Peptidoglycan"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thick, many layers (about 50 percent of wall)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thin, one to a few layers (5–10 percent)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teichoic acids"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present — major surface antigen"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Absent"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outer membrane"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Absent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present, with porins"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Absent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present — lipid A plus O antigen"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Periplasmic space"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Narrow or absent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prominent, contains beta-lactamases"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipoprotein"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Absent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Present, anchoring outer membrane to peptidoglycan"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Toxins of note"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exotoxins (proteins, mostly)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Endotoxin, plus exotoxins in some species"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinically Important Genera"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive cocci:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Staphylococcus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — clusters, catalase positive. S. aureus (coagulase"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"positive: abscesses, endotoxin-negative toxic shock, food poisoning);   coagulase-negative Staphylococcus epidermidis (line and prosthetic   infections) and S. saprophyticus (urinary tract infection in young women)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Streptococcus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — chains, catalase negative: S. pyogenes (group A —"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"pharyngitis, rheumatic fever, nephritis), S. agalactiae (group B —   neonatal sepsis), S. pneumoniae (pneumonia, otitis, meningitis),   viridans group (endocarditis), Enterococcus (see VRE below)."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Enterococcus faecalis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"E. faecium","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — intrinsic resistance to many"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"drugs, gut and hospital flora."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive bacilli:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Bacillus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (anthrax), "},{"type":"text","text":"Clostridium","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (tetanus, botulism, gas gangrene, pseudomembranous colitis), "},{"type":"text","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Listeria monocytogenes","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — with "},{"type":"text","text":"Nocardia","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" branching and partially acid-fast."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-negative cocci:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Neisseria meningitidis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"N. gonorrhoeae","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the diplococci."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-negative bacilli:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" the Enterobacteriaceae (E. coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus, Enterobacter, Serratia) — glucose fermenters; plus the non-fermenters "},{"type":"text","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (opportunistic, burns, cystic fibrosis, intrinsically resistant) and "},{"type":"text","text":"Acinetobacter","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; the fastidious coccobacilli "},{"type":"text","text":"Haemophilus influenzae","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Bordetella pertussis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; the curved and spiral "},{"type":"text","text":"Vibrio cholerae","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Campylobacter","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Helicobacter pylori","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; the zoonoses "},{"type":"text","text":"Brucella","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Francisella","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Yersinia pestis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; and the anaerobe "},{"type":"text","text":"Bacteroides fragilis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Organisms that dodge the Gram stain:","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and leprae (acid-fast by Ziehl-Neelsen — mycolic acids), "},{"type":"text","text":"Mycoplasma","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (no cell wall), "},{"type":"text","text":"Treponema pallidum","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Leptospira","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (too thin to see by light microscopy — darkfield or serology), "},{"type":"text","text":"Chlamydia","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Rickettsia","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (intracellular, stain poorly)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Treatment Implications"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wall decides which drugs can even arrive at their targets:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Beta-lactams","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems) block"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"peptidoglycan cross-linking and work on both groups — but gram-negative   enzymes matter. "},{"type":"text","text":"Staphylococcus aureus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" penicillinase means antistaph   penicillins (flucloxacillin, nafcillin); Enterobacteriaceae produce TEM   and SHV beta-lactamases in the periplasm, and their ESBL and   carbapenemase-expressing descendants define the "},{"type":"text","text":"antimicrobial resistance   topic hub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/topics/antimicrobial-resistance/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vancomycin","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — binds the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus but is too bulky for"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"gram-negative porins: gram-positive only."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — entry routes differ; gram-negative"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"uptake of aminoglycosides depends on oxygen-dependent transport."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Polymyxins","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (colistin) — act on the gram-negative outer membrane,"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"damaging LPS; essentially gram-negative only, and a last resort against   carbapenem-resistant strains."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Endotoxin release on therapy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — rapid killing of gram-negative bacteria"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"can release LPS and transiently worsen the inflammatory picture; clinically   managed supportively rather than by drug choice."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"MRSA and VRE"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MRSA","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"text","text":"S. aureus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with mecA-encoded PBP2a (also called"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PBP2''), which has low affinity for all conventional beta-lactams.   Healthcare-associated strains are often multidrug resistant; community   strains often retain susceptibility to clindamycin and   trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Definitive therapy: intravenous vancomycin   (with trough monitoring), daptomycin, linezolid, or ceftaroline, which   uniquely binds PBP2a."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VRE","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — enterococci, overwhelmingly "},{"type":"text","text":"E. faecium","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", in which van genes"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(vanA the classic) replace the D-Ala-D-Ala peptide terminus with   D-Ala-D-Lac, removing vancomycin''s docking site. Linezolid and daptomycin   are the usual agents."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cruel irony: vancomycin resistance moving from enterococci into"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Staphylococcus aureus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" has produced rare VRSA strains — the reason   stewardship is a patient-safety issue, not paperwork."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worked exam framing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A vignette: "},{"type":"text","text":" pus from an abscess shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase positive, coagulase positive; the patient has a history of repeated hospital admissions.","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" That is "},{"type":"text","text":"S. aureus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and the admission history flags MRSA risk — empirical flucloxacillin is wrong; vancomycin or another MRSA-active agent is needed until susceptibilities return. Change \"clusters\" to \"chains, catalase negative\" and think "},{"type":"text","text":"Streptococcus","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":"; change the smear to \"gram-negative diplococci\" in cerebrospinal fluid and think "},{"type":"text","text":"N. meningitidis","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with ceftriaxone immediately — the stain reading drives the first dose."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revision Pointers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rehearse the stain steps until you can explain, not recite, why purple stays purple. Then drill the genera lists by shape and stain — they are pure marks. Finish with the "},{"type":"text","text":"microbiology practice quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/microbiology/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and keep the resistance mechanisms in one table, because MRSA, VRE, ESBL and CRE are the four acronyms that carry the modern exam."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Learning objectives"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Describe the Gram staining procedure and the chemistry that distinguishes the two groups"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare gram-positive and gram-negative cell wall architecture structure by structure"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List the clinically important gram-positive and gram-negative genera"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explain how cell wall differences shape antibiotic choice and susceptibility"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Describe the mechanism and treatment of MRSA and VRE"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recognize the bacteria that do not stain by the Gram method and the techniques used instead"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key terms"}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Peptidoglycan","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The rigid polymer of N-acetylglucosamine and N-acetylmuramic acid cross-linked by peptide bridges that gives the bacterial wall its strength — the target of beta-lactams and vancomycin."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teichoic acid","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Antigenic polymers woven through the gram-positive peptidoglycan; the main surface antigen of gram-positive cocci."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipopolysaccharide (LPS)","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Endotoxin of the gram-negative outer membrane — lipid A triggers the septic shock cascade; the O antigen types enteric bacteria."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outer membrane","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The extra lipid bilayer of gram-negative bacteria, studded with porins and a barrier to many antibiotics and dyes."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Periplasmic space","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The compartment between the gram-negative inner and outer membranes that houses beta-lactamases and other degrading enzymes."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MRSA","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, carrying the mecA gene for an altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a) resistant to nearly all beta-lactams."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VRE","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Vancomycin-resistant enterococci, most often Enterococcus faecium carrying vanA, which replaces the D-Ala-D-Ala target with D-Ala-D-Lac."}]}]},{"type":"definition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Decolorizer","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The alcohol-acetone step of the Gram stain that shrinks the gram-positive pore network and washes the dye out of gram-negative cells."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Important points"}]},{"type":"important","content":[{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive bacteria retain crystal violet because a thick peptidoglycan layer traps the crystal violet-iodine complex; gram-negative cells lose it through their thin wall and dissolving outer membrane."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only gram-negative bacteria carry lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) — the mediator of gram-negative septic shock."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The gram-negative outer membrane with its porins is a permeability barrier that excludes many large antibiotics such as vancomycin."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gram-positive cocci include Staphylococcus, Streptococcus and Enterococcus; the gram-negative diplococci are the Neisseria."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Enterobacteriaceae (E. coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus) are gram-negative bacilli that ferment glucose."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mycobacteria are neither — their waxy mycolic-acid wall demands the acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen) stain; Mycoplasma has no cell wall at all."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MRSA is treated with vancomycin, daptomycin or linezolid; VRE with linezolid or daptomycin — the cross-resistance problem of the two."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"keyPoint","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Gram stain divides bacteria into two wall architectures. Gram-positive organisms have a thick peptidoglycan layer with teichoic acids and no outer membrane, so they retain the crystal violet-iodine complex and stain purple. Gram-negative organisms have a thin peptidoglycan between an inner membrane and an outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide, so the dye is decolorized and they take the pink counterstain. The structural difference drives clinical reality: endotoxin causes gram-negative shock, the outer membrane blocks drugs like vancomycin, periplasmic beta-lactamases undermine penicillins, and the flagship resistance problems — MRSA and VRE — sit on the gram-positive side while ESBL and carbapenemase producers sit on the gram-negative side."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why do gram-positive bacteria retain the crystal violet stain?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The crystal violet-iodine complex is large, and the thick, multi-layered peptidoglycan of gram-positive cells dehydrates and shrinks during alcohol decolorization, trapping the complex inside. Gram-negative walls are thin and their outer membrane dissolves in the decolorizer, so the complex washes out and the safranin counterstain colors them pink."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which component of the gram-negative cell wall causes septic shock?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lipopolysaccharide — specifically its lipid A moiety. It binds TLR4 on monocytes and macrophages, releasing tumor necrosis factor and interleukin-1, which drive fever, vasodilation, capillary leak and refractory hypotension. Gram-positive bacteria lack LPS, though their teichoic acids can trigger a milder, TLR2-mediated response."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why is vancomycin ineffective against gram-negative bacteria?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vancomycin is a large molecule that cannot pass through the porins of the gram-negative outer membrane, so it never reaches its peptidoglycan precursor target in the periplasm. It is active only against gram-positive organisms — which is exactly why gram-positive resistance to it, as in VRE, is such a problem."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the mechanism of methicillin resistance in MRSA?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The mecA gene encodes penicillin-binding protein 2a (PBP2a), a transpeptidase with low affinity for nearly all beta-lactam antibiotics, so cell wall synthesis continues in their presence. Treatment options are vancomycin, daptomycin, linezolid, or ceftaroline, the one beta-lactam that binds PBP2a."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Jawetz, Melnick & Adelberg''s Medical Microbiology","source":"McGraw Hill"},{"title":"Prescott''s Microbiology","source":"McGraw Hill"},{"title":"Murray, Rosenthal, Pfaller: Medical Microbiology","source":"Elsevier"}]}}]}',
  '<h2>Overview</h2><p>These notes contrast the gram-positive and gram-negative cell wall, explain the chemistry behind Gram staining, list the medically important genera on each side, and draw out the treatment and resistance implications — endotoxin, beta-lactamases, MRSA and VRE.</p><h2>Introduction</h2><p>The Gram stain is the oldest and still the fastest bench test in bacteriology: a smear from a colony or a specimen, four reagents, and within minutes you know which of two cell wall architectures you are facing — which shortens the antibiotic list before any culture grows. These notes cover the stain, the walls that explain it, the genera that matter, and the resistance story that continues in the <a href="/topics/antimicrobial-resistance/" rel="noopener">antimicrobial resistance topic hub</a>.</p><h2>The Gram Stain and Why It Works</h2><p>Four steps, and the chemistry is the exam answer:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Crystal violet</strong> — the primary stain; all cells take it up.</p></li><li><p><strong>Gram''s iodine</strong> — a mordant that forms the large crystal violet-iodine</p></li></ol><p>complex inside the cell.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Alcohol-acetone decolorizer</strong> — the decisive step. In gram-positive</p></li></ol><p>cells the thick peptidoglycan dehydrates, its pores shrink, and the    complex is trapped. In gram-negative cells the thin wall plus the    dissolving outer membrane lets the complex wash out.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Safranin</strong> — the pink counterstain that colors the now-colorless</p></li></ol><p>gram-negative cells.</p><p><strong>Gram-positive stain purple; gram-negative stain pink.</strong> The staining difference is wall architecture, not membrane chemistry of the dye itself — a distinction examiners like to press.</p><h2>Cell Wall Architecture</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Structure</p></th><th><p>Gram-positive</p></th><th><p>Gram-negative</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Peptidoglycan</p></td><td><p>Thick, many layers (about 50 percent of wall)</p></td><td><p>Thin, one to a few layers (5–10 percent)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Teichoic acids</p></td><td><p>Present — major surface antigen</p></td><td><p>Absent</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Outer membrane</p></td><td><p>Absent</p></td><td><p>Present, with porins</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin)</p></td><td><p>Absent</p></td><td><p>Present — lipid A plus O antigen</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Periplasmic space</p></td><td><p>Narrow or absent</p></td><td><p>Prominent, contains beta-lactamases</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Lipoprotein</p></td><td><p>Absent</p></td><td><p>Present, anchoring outer membrane to peptidoglycan</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Toxins of note</p></td><td><p>Exotoxins (proteins, mostly)</p></td><td><p>Endotoxin, plus exotoxins in some species</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Clinically Important Genera</h2><p><strong>Gram-positive cocci:</strong></p><ul><li><p><em>Staphylococcus</em> — clusters, catalase positive. S. aureus (coagulase</p></li></ul><p>positive: abscesses, endotoxin-negative toxic shock, food poisoning);   coagulase-negative Staphylococcus epidermidis (line and prosthetic   infections) and S. saprophyticus (urinary tract infection in young women).</p><ul><li><p><em>Streptococcus</em> — chains, catalase negative: S. pyogenes (group A —</p></li></ul><p>pharyngitis, rheumatic fever, nephritis), S. agalactiae (group B —   neonatal sepsis), S. pneumoniae (pneumonia, otitis, meningitis),   viridans group (endocarditis), Enterococcus (see VRE below).</p><ul><li><p><em>Enterococcus faecalis</em> and <em>E. faecium</em> — intrinsic resistance to many</p></li></ul><p>drugs, gut and hospital flora.</p><p><strong>Gram-positive bacilli:</strong> <em>Bacillus</em> (anthrax), <em>Clostridium</em> (tetanus, botulism, gas gangrene, pseudomembranous colitis), <em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae</em>, <em>Listeria monocytogenes</em> — with <em>Nocardia</em> branching and partially acid-fast.</p><p><strong>Gram-negative cocci:</strong> <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em> and <em>N. gonorrhoeae</em> — the diplococci.</p><p><strong>Gram-negative bacilli:</strong> the Enterobacteriaceae (E. coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus, Enterobacter, Serratia) — glucose fermenters; plus the non-fermenters <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> (opportunistic, burns, cystic fibrosis, intrinsically resistant) and <em>Acinetobacter</em>; the fastidious coccobacilli <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> and <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>; the curved and spiral <em>Vibrio cholerae</em>, <em>Campylobacter</em>, <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>; the zoonoses <em>Brucella</em>, <em>Francisella</em>, <em>Yersinia pestis</em>; and the anaerobe <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</p><p><strong>Organisms that dodge the Gram stain:</strong> <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> and leprae (acid-fast by Ziehl-Neelsen — mycolic acids), <em>Mycoplasma</em> (no cell wall), <em>Treponema pallidum</em> and <em>Leptospira</em> (too thin to see by light microscopy — darkfield or serology), <em>Chlamydia</em> and <em>Rickettsia</em> (intracellular, stain poorly).</p><h2>Treatment Implications</h2><p>The wall decides which drugs can even arrive at their targets:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Beta-lactams</strong> (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems) block</p></li></ul><p>peptidoglycan cross-linking and work on both groups — but gram-negative   enzymes matter. <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> penicillinase means antistaph   penicillins (flucloxacillin, nafcillin); Enterobacteriaceae produce TEM   and SHV beta-lactamases in the periplasm, and their ESBL and   carbapenemase-expressing descendants define the <a href="/topics/antimicrobial-resistance/" rel="noopener">antimicrobial resistance   topic hub</a>.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Vancomycin</strong> — binds the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus but is too bulky for</p></li></ul><p>gram-negative porins: gram-positive only.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines</strong> — entry routes differ; gram-negative</p></li></ul><p>uptake of aminoglycosides depends on oxygen-dependent transport.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Polymyxins</strong> (colistin) — act on the gram-negative outer membrane,</p></li></ul><p>damaging LPS; essentially gram-negative only, and a last resort against   carbapenem-resistant strains.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Endotoxin release on therapy</strong> — rapid killing of gram-negative bacteria</p></li></ul><p>can release LPS and transiently worsen the inflammatory picture; clinically   managed supportively rather than by drug choice.</p><h2>MRSA and VRE</h2><ul><li><p><strong>MRSA</strong> — <em>S. aureus</em> with mecA-encoded PBP2a (also called</p></li></ul><p>PBP2''), which has low affinity for all conventional beta-lactams.   Healthcare-associated strains are often multidrug resistant; community   strains often retain susceptibility to clindamycin and   trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Definitive therapy: intravenous vancomycin   (with trough monitoring), daptomycin, linezolid, or ceftaroline, which   uniquely binds PBP2a.</p><ul><li><p><strong>VRE</strong> — enterococci, overwhelmingly <em>E. faecium</em>, in which van genes</p></li></ul><p>(vanA the classic) replace the D-Ala-D-Ala peptide terminus with   D-Ala-D-Lac, removing vancomycin''s docking site. Linezolid and daptomycin   are the usual agents.</p><ul><li><p>The cruel irony: vancomycin resistance moving from enterococci into</p></li></ul><p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> has produced rare VRSA strains — the reason   stewardship is a patient-safety issue, not paperwork.</p><h3>Worked exam framing</h3><p>A vignette: <em> pus from an abscess shows gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase positive, coagulase positive; the patient has a history of repeated hospital admissions.</em> That is <em>S. aureus</em>, and the admission history flags MRSA risk — empirical flucloxacillin is wrong; vancomycin or another MRSA-active agent is needed until susceptibilities return. Change "clusters" to "chains, catalase negative" and think <em>Streptococcus</em>; change the smear to "gram-negative diplococci" in cerebrospinal fluid and think <em>N. meningitidis</em> with ceftriaxone immediately — the stain reading drives the first dose.</p><h2>Revision Pointers</h2><p>Rehearse the stain steps until you can explain, not recite, why purple stays purple. Then drill the genera lists by shape and stain — they are pure marks. Finish with the <a href="/practice/quiz/microbiology/" rel="noopener">microbiology practice quiz</a> and keep the resistance mechanisms in one table, because MRSA, VRE, ESBL and CRE are the four acronyms that carry the modern exam.</p><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li><p>Describe the Gram staining procedure and the chemistry that distinguishes the two groups</p></li><li><p>Compare gram-positive and gram-negative cell wall architecture structure by structure</p></li><li><p>List the clinically important gram-positive and gram-negative genera</p></li><li><p>Explain how cell wall differences shape antibiotic choice and susceptibility</p></li><li><p>Describe the mechanism and treatment of MRSA and VRE</p></li><li><p>Recognize the bacteria that do not stain by the Gram method and the techniques used instead</p></li></ul><h2>Key terms</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Peptidoglycan</strong> — The rigid polymer of N-acetylglucosamine and N-acetylmuramic acid cross-linked by peptide bridges that gives the bacterial wall its strength — the target of beta-lactams and vancomycin.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Teichoic acid</strong> — Antigenic polymers woven through the gram-positive peptidoglycan; the main surface antigen of gram-positive cocci.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Lipopolysaccharide (LPS)</strong> — Endotoxin of the gram-negative outer membrane — lipid A triggers the septic shock cascade; the O antigen types enteric bacteria.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Outer membrane</strong> — The extra lipid bilayer of gram-negative bacteria, studded with porins and a barrier to many antibiotics and dyes.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Periplasmic space</strong> — The compartment between the gram-negative inner and outer membranes that houses beta-lactamases and other degrading enzymes.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>MRSA</strong> — Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, carrying the mecA gene for an altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a) resistant to nearly all beta-lactams.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>VRE</strong> — Vancomycin-resistant enterococci, most often Enterococcus faecium carrying vanA, which replaces the D-Ala-D-Ala target with D-Ala-D-Lac.</p></aside><aside class="msn-block msn-definition" data-type="definition"><p class="msn-block-label">Definition</p><p><strong>Decolorizer</strong> — The alcohol-acetone step of the Gram stain that shrinks the gram-positive pore network and washes the dye out of gram-negative cells.</p></aside><h2>Important points</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-important" data-type="important"><p class="msn-block-label">Important</p><ul><li><p>Gram-positive bacteria retain crystal violet because a thick peptidoglycan layer traps the crystal violet-iodine complex; gram-negative cells lose it through their thin wall and dissolving outer membrane.</p></li><li><p>Only gram-negative bacteria carry lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin) — the mediator of gram-negative septic shock.</p></li><li><p>The gram-negative outer membrane with its porins is a permeability barrier that excludes many large antibiotics such as vancomycin.</p></li><li><p>Gram-positive cocci include Staphylococcus, Streptococcus and Enterococcus; the gram-negative diplococci are the Neisseria.</p></li><li><p>The Enterobacteriaceae (E. coli, Klebsiella, Salmonella, Shigella, Proteus) are gram-negative bacilli that ferment glucose.</p></li><li><p>Mycobacteria are neither — their waxy mycolic-acid wall demands the acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen) stain; Mycoplasma has no cell wall at all.</p></li><li><p>MRSA is treated with vancomycin, daptomycin or linezolid; VRE with linezolid or daptomycin — the cross-resistance problem of the two.</p></li></ul></aside><h2>Summary</h2><aside class="msn-block msn-key-point" data-type="key-point"><p class="msn-block-label">Key point</p><p>The Gram stain divides bacteria into two wall architectures. Gram-positive organisms have a thick peptidoglycan layer with teichoic acids and no outer membrane, so they retain the crystal violet-iodine complex and stain purple. Gram-negative organisms have a thin peptidoglycan between an inner membrane and an outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide, so the dye is decolorized and they take the pink counterstain. The structural difference drives clinical reality: endotoxin causes gram-negative shock, the outer membrane blocks drugs like vancomycin, periplasmic beta-lactamases undermine penicillins, and the flagship resistance problems — MRSA and VRE — sit on the gram-positive side while ESBL and carbapenemase producers sit on the gram-negative side.</p></aside><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Why do gram-positive bacteria retain the crystal violet stain?</h3><p>The crystal violet-iodine complex is large, and the thick, multi-layered peptidoglycan of gram-positive cells dehydrates and shrinks during alcohol decolorization, trapping the complex inside. Gram-negative walls are thin and their outer membrane dissolves in the decolorizer, so the complex washes out and the safranin counterstain colors them pink.</p><h3>Which component of the gram-negative cell wall causes septic shock?</h3><p>Lipopolysaccharide — specifically its lipid A moiety. It binds TLR4 on monocytes and macrophages, releasing tumor necrosis factor and interleukin-1, which drive fever, vasodilation, capillary leak and refractory hypotension. Gram-positive bacteria lack LPS, though their teichoic acids can trigger a milder, TLR2-mediated response.</p><h3>Why is vancomycin ineffective against gram-negative bacteria?</h3><p>Vancomycin is a large molecule that cannot pass through the porins of the gram-negative outer membrane, so it never reaches its peptidoglycan precursor target in the periplasm. It is active only against gram-positive organisms — which is exactly why gram-positive resistance to it, as in VRE, is such a problem.</p><h3>What is the mechanism of methicillin resistance in MRSA?</h3><p>The mecA gene encodes penicillin-binding protein 2a (PBP2a), a transpeptidase with low affinity for nearly all beta-lactam antibiotics, so cell wall synthesis continues in their presence. Treatment options are vancomycin, daptomycin, linezolid, or ceftaroline, the one beta-lactam that binds PBP2a.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Jawetz, Melnick &amp; Adelberg''s Medical Microbiology. McGraw Hill</li><li>Prescott''s Microbiology. McGraw Hill</li><li>Murray, Rosenthal, Pfaller: Medical Microbiology. Elsevier</li></ol></aside>',
