INSERT INTO articles (
  title, slug, excerpt, content_json, content_html, article_type, status, subject_id, topic_id, author_id, published_at, seo_title, meta_description, robots, reading_minutes, created_at, updated_at, category
) VALUES (
  'The MCQ Mistakes That Cost Marks in Pathology Exams',
  'pathology-mcq-mistakes',
  'Seven MCQ mistakes that cost marks in pathology exams, from buzzword pattern-matching to most-common versus most-deadly stems, each with the wrong instinct and the fix.',
  '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pathology MCQ papers are generous to prepared students and merciless to prepared students who answer badly. The difference is usually not knowledge but exam behavior. These are the seven mistakes that cost the most marks, with the wrong instinct behind each, why it fails, and the fix that recovers the marks."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Buzzword Pattern-Matching Without Reading the Stem"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: \"caseating granulomas, I know this one,\" and the answer is marked before the third line of the stem is read. Why it fails: examiners deliberately plant buzzwords alongside a detail that changes the answer, an immune status, a duration, a location, an age. The stem that says \"caseating granuloma in a 45-year-old, sputum positive\" and the one that says \"caseating granuloma in a corticosteroid-treated patient\" can have different best answers. The fix: read the last sentence of the stem first, so you know exactly what is being asked, then read the entire stem, then answer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Confusing Necrosis Types by Location"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: matching a necrosis type to an organ from memory of a list, without thinking about the mechanism. Why it fails: the associations only stick when you know why. Coagulative necrosis is the default in ischemic infarcts of solid organs because the architecture is ghost-preserved, except the brain, where liquefactive necrosis dominates because of the high lipid content and enzymatic environment. Caseous necrosis means mycobacterial or fungal infection, a semi-solid debris behind a granuloma, as covered in the "},{"type":"text","text":"cell injury note","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/notes/cell-injury/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":". Fat necrosis follows pancreatic enzyme release, and fibrinoid necrosis marks immune-mediated vessel damage. The fix: learn each type with its mechanism, and each mechanism with its one-line why. The location list then becomes impossible to forget."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Missing \"Most Common\" Versus \"Most Deadly\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: answering the disease that left the strongest impression. Why it fails: \"most common\" and \"most often fatal\" are different questions with different answers, and examiners set both in the same paper precisely to catch this. Osteosarcoma and osteochondroma habitually swap places this way, as do papillary and anaplastic thyroid carcinoma, the first common and indolent, the second rare and lethal. The fix: circle the qualifier in the stem before answering. Underline \"most common\", \"most deadly\", \"most likely\", \"least likely\" every time, and build separate lists for each qualifier when you revise."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Skipping the \"Except\", \"Least\", and \"Not\" Qualifiers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: reading four true statements and marking the first one. Why it fails: the question asks for the false statement, and the first true option is a deliberate trap for exactly this reading style. The fix: same habit as above, but stricter. Any negative qualifier gets underlined twice. If your university''s papers allow rough work, write a minus sign next to negative stems before looking at options."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Choosing the Diagnosis Before Reading All Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: seeing the correct diagnosis at option B and marking it without reading C and D. Why it fails: one-best-answer papers sometimes list two correct-sounding options where only one matches the specific question asked, for example the finding that best explains a laboratory value versus the most likely diagnosis. The fix: read every option. It costs two seconds each and recovers marks every paper."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Revising by Rereading, Not Retrieving"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: \"I will do questions after I finish the syllabus.\" Why it fails: rereading inflates recognition, which feels like mastery and collapses in the hall, where the task is retrieval under time pressure. The fix: after every topic, attempt MCQs on it immediately, even with notes half-finished, and use every wrong answer as a pointer back to the relevant section of Robbins. The wrong answers are your syllabus."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Guessing Without Eliminating"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The wrong instinct: leaving a difficult question blank, or picking by gut feel. Why it fails: negative marking or not, a considered guess after eliminating two options beats an unconsidered one. The fix: for every hard stem, eliminate the options you can disprove, then choose between what remains. Mark the question for review and move on, so one hard stem never eats four easy ones."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern behind all seven is the same: the exam tests careful production under constraint, not familiarity. Practice under timed conditions with the "},{"type":"text","text":"pathology quiz","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/practice/quiz/pathology/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", review every option of every question, and the technique becomes as automatic as the knowledge."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequently asked questions"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I read Robbins cover to cover before attempting MCQs?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Reading and testing should interleave from the start. Attempting MCQs after each topic, even badly, is a form of retrieval practice that strengthens the reading. Students who save questions for the end lose both the learning benefit and the diagnostic information about their weak chapters."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many pathology MCQs should I do before professionals?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quality beats volume, but a realistic floor is around two thousand questions across the subject, done with review of every option, not just the key. A question you got wrong and understood is worth more than five you got right on a guess."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is it better to change an answer after marking it?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only change with a concrete reason, a remembered fact or a stem detail you misread. Gut-feel reversals tend to move answers from correct to incorrect. If your only reason is uneasiness, leave the original answer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I stop falling for buzzword answers?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the full stem, form your own answer before looking at the options, and treat every buzzword in the options as a distractor until the whole clinical picture is checked against it. Examiners know which buzzwords students anchor on."}]},{"type":"references","attrs":{"items":[{"title":"Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease (Kumar, Abbas, Aster)","source":"Elsevier"},{"title":"Fundamentals of Pathology (Pathoma) — Husain Sattar","source":"Pathoma"},{"title":"Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning (Brown, Roediger, McDaniel)","source":"Harvard University Press"}]}}]}',
  '<p>Pathology MCQ papers are generous to prepared students and merciless to prepared students who answer badly. The difference is usually not knowledge but exam behavior. These are the seven mistakes that cost the most marks, with the wrong instinct behind each, why it fails, and the fix that recovers the marks.</p><h2>1. Buzzword Pattern-Matching Without Reading the Stem</h2><p>The wrong instinct: "caseating granulomas, I know this one," and the answer is marked before the third line of the stem is read. Why it fails: examiners deliberately plant buzzwords alongside a detail that changes the answer, an immune status, a duration, a location, an age. The stem that says "caseating granuloma in a 45-year-old, sputum positive" and the one that says "caseating granuloma in a corticosteroid-treated patient" can have different best answers. The fix: read the last sentence of the stem first, so you know exactly what is being asked, then read the entire stem, then answer.</p><h2>2. Confusing Necrosis Types by Location</h2><p>The wrong instinct: matching a necrosis type to an organ from memory of a list, without thinking about the mechanism. Why it fails: the associations only stick when you know why. Coagulative necrosis is the default in ischemic infarcts of solid organs because the architecture is ghost-preserved, except the brain, where liquefactive necrosis dominates because of the high lipid content and enzymatic environment. Caseous necrosis means mycobacterial or fungal infection, a semi-solid debris behind a granuloma, as covered in the <a href="/notes/cell-injury/" rel="noopener">cell injury note</a>. Fat necrosis follows pancreatic enzyme release, and fibrinoid necrosis marks immune-mediated vessel damage. The fix: learn each type with its mechanism, and each mechanism with its one-line why. The location list then becomes impossible to forget.</p><h2>3. Missing "Most Common" Versus "Most Deadly"</h2><p>The wrong instinct: answering the disease that left the strongest impression. Why it fails: "most common" and "most often fatal" are different questions with different answers, and examiners set both in the same paper precisely to catch this. Osteosarcoma and osteochondroma habitually swap places this way, as do papillary and anaplastic thyroid carcinoma, the first common and indolent, the second rare and lethal. The fix: circle the qualifier in the stem before answering. Underline "most common", "most deadly", "most likely", "least likely" every time, and build separate lists for each qualifier when you revise.</p><h2>4. Skipping the "Except", "Least", and "Not" Qualifiers</h2><p>The wrong instinct: reading four true statements and marking the first one. Why it fails: the question asks for the false statement, and the first true option is a deliberate trap for exactly this reading style. The fix: same habit as above, but stricter. Any negative qualifier gets underlined twice. If your university''s papers allow rough work, write a minus sign next to negative stems before looking at options.</p><h2>5. Choosing the Diagnosis Before Reading All Options</h2><p>The wrong instinct: seeing the correct diagnosis at option B and marking it without reading C and D. Why it fails: one-best-answer papers sometimes list two correct-sounding options where only one matches the specific question asked, for example the finding that best explains a laboratory value versus the most likely diagnosis. The fix: read every option. It costs two seconds each and recovers marks every paper.</p><h2>6. Revising by Rereading, Not Retrieving</h2><p>The wrong instinct: "I will do questions after I finish the syllabus." Why it fails: rereading inflates recognition, which feels like mastery and collapses in the hall, where the task is retrieval under time pressure. The fix: after every topic, attempt MCQs on it immediately, even with notes half-finished, and use every wrong answer as a pointer back to the relevant section of Robbins. The wrong answers are your syllabus.</p><h2>7. Guessing Without Eliminating</h2><p>The wrong instinct: leaving a difficult question blank, or picking by gut feel. Why it fails: negative marking or not, a considered guess after eliminating two options beats an unconsidered one. The fix: for every hard stem, eliminate the options you can disprove, then choose between what remains. Mark the question for review and move on, so one hard stem never eats four easy ones.</p><p>The pattern behind all seven is the same: the exam tests careful production under constraint, not familiarity. Practice under timed conditions with the <a href="/practice/quiz/pathology/" rel="noopener">pathology quiz</a>, review every option of every question, and the technique becomes as automatic as the knowledge.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Should I read Robbins cover to cover before attempting MCQs?</h3><p>No. Reading and testing should interleave from the start. Attempting MCQs after each topic, even badly, is a form of retrieval practice that strengthens the reading. Students who save questions for the end lose both the learning benefit and the diagnostic information about their weak chapters.</p><h3>How many pathology MCQs should I do before professionals?</h3><p>Quality beats volume, but a realistic floor is around two thousand questions across the subject, done with review of every option, not just the key. A question you got wrong and understood is worth more than five you got right on a guess.</p><h3>Is it better to change an answer after marking it?</h3><p>Only change with a concrete reason, a remembered fact or a stem detail you misread. Gut-feel reversals tend to move answers from correct to incorrect. If your only reason is uneasiness, leave the original answer.</p><h3>How do I stop falling for buzzword answers?</h3><p>Read the full stem, form your own answer before looking at the options, and treat every buzzword in the options as a distractor until the whole clinical picture is checked against it. Examiners know which buzzwords students anchor on.</p><aside class="msn-block msn-references" data-type="references"><p class="msn-block-label">References</p><ol><li>Robbins &amp; Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease (Kumar, Abbas, Aster). Elsevier</li><li>Fundamentals of Pathology (Pathoma) — Husain Sattar. Pathoma</li><li>Make It Stick: The Science of Successful Learning (Brown, Roediger, McDaniel). Harvard University Press</li></ol></aside>',
  'article',
  'published',
  NULL,
  NULL,
  (SELECT id FROM users ORDER BY id LIMIT 1),
  '2026-08-28 09:00:00',
  NULL,
  'Seven MCQ mistakes that cost marks in pathology exams, from buzzword pattern-matching to most-common versus most-deadly stems, each with the wrong instinct and the fix.',
  'index,follow',
